-Look closely, Martos.

261
00:09:57,332 --> 00:09:59,300
Have you ever seen
such people before?

262
00:09:59,333 --> 00:09:59,833
-No.

263
00:09:59,867 --> 00:10:00,835
-Everybody skip!

264
00:10:00,868 --> 00:10:02,703
-But I betcha they're in
league with Unga Khan.

265
00:10:02,737 --> 00:10:03,270
-Unga Khan.

266
00:10:03,303 --> 00:10:04,438
-Is that Matt Dillon?

267
00:10:06,907 --> 00:10:07,608
-Hi!

268
00:10:07,642 --> 00:10:08,808
-Which one of us
is he waving at?

269
00:10:12,747 --> 00:10:15,282
-Follow me!

270
00:10:15,315 --> 00:10:17,552
-Oh, it's Jim Henson's
Birth of a Nation babies.

271
00:10:17,585 --> 00:10:20,520
-Oh.

272
00:10:20,553 --> 00:10:21,555
-(MOCKINGLY) Follow me!

273
00:10:24,892 --> 00:10:26,793
-Look at all those
horses under the sea.

274
00:10:26,827 --> 00:10:28,495
-So how do they get
them under the sea?

275
00:10:28,528 --> 00:10:29,730
-Sea horses?

276
00:10:29,730 --> 00:10:30,330
-D'oh!

277
00:10:30,330 --> 00:10:32,900
Oh!

278
00:10:32,933 --> 00:10:33,833
-Ben Hur.

279
00:10:33,867 --> 00:10:34,368
-Ben Hecht.

280
00:10:34,402 --> 00:10:35,737
-Charles MacArthur.

281
00:10:35,770 --> 00:10:37,572
-Jimmy Smits.

282
00:10:37,605 --> 00:10:38,705
-Ah, here's the stop.

283
00:10:38,738 --> 00:10:39,607
Everybody get transferred.

284
00:10:42,443 --> 00:10:44,312
-[Hums along with
background music]

285
00:10:44,345 --> 00:10:46,713
-OK, if things get tough,
we'll offer 'em Billy, agreed?

286
00:10:46,747 --> 00:10:47,313
-Cool.

287
00:10:47,348 --> 00:10:47,748
-Agreed.

288
00:10:51,652 --> 00:10:54,622
-Oh, it's like the end of
a high speed chase in LA.

289
00:10:54,655 --> 00:10:56,790
-Ride back to the
Sacred City and report

290
00:10:56,823 --> 00:10:58,358
to Sharad, High
Priest of Atlantis,

291
00:10:58,392 --> 00:11:00,627
that we are returning
with strange prisoners.

292
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:01,662
-Weird prisoners.

293
00:11:07,167 --> 00:11:10,503
-(SHRILLY) I wanna
ride with Diana!

294
00:11:10,537 --> 00:11:12,640
-Uh, you're a little late
for the posse, fella!

295
00:11:15,442 --> 00:11:16,643
-(SIMULTANEOUSLY)
Da da-da-da-da,

296
00:11:16,677 --> 00:11:20,380
da-da-da, da-da-da-da da.

297
00:11:20,413 --> 00:11:22,783
-The end.

298
00:11:22,817 --> 00:11:24,017
-Oh.

299
00:11:24,052 --> 00:11:25,987
-With Mark Spitz,
Jimmy Smits, and Rossy,

300
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:27,488
the French wonder horse.

301
00:11:27,522 --> 00:11:27,988
-Open the gate!

302
00:11:28,022 --> 00:11:29,090
-It's Martos.

303
00:11:29,123 --> 00:11:32,393
-Open the gate and make
some summer sausage, quick!

304
00:11:32,427 --> 00:11:33,727
-Too late.

305
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:36,597
-Ouch.

306
00:11:36,630 --> 00:11:37,565
-Open the back door!

307
00:11:37,598 --> 00:11:39,933
He can't stop!

308
00:11:39,967 --> 00:11:42,770
-Here, wash and wax this.

309
00:11:42,803 --> 00:11:44,037
-Thank you, sir.

310
00:11:44,072 --> 00:11:47,642
Ah, jeez, all I got is a crummy
popcorn ball and some Jujubes.

311
00:11:47,675 --> 00:11:50,110
-Looks like a set The
Monkeys would end up on.

312
00:11:53,513 --> 00:11:54,582
-Confident, dry, and secure.

313
00:11:54,615 --> 00:11:57,518
-Darius approaches the
city with strange captives.

314
00:11:57,552 --> 00:11:58,818
-Strange captives?

315
00:11:58,852 --> 00:11:59,453
-Weird captives.

316
00:11:59,487 --> 00:11:59,887
Kept

317
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:00,855
-Where are they?

318
00:12:00,888 --> 00:12:03,690
-We believe them to be
agents of Unga Khan.

319
00:12:03,723 --> 00:12:04,458
-Unga Khan.

320
00:12:04,492 --> 00:12:05,325
-That sucks.

321
00:12:05,358 --> 00:12:07,595
-There will be no
peace in Atlantis.

322
00:12:07,628 --> 00:12:09,663
-Can we break your hat open
now and eat the popcorn?

323
00:12:09,697 --> 00:12:14,102
-Power of this evil
usurper, Unga Khan.

324
00:12:14,135 --> 00:12:16,903
-Anderson Windows
and Wall-- Doors.

325
00:12:16,937 --> 00:12:19,072
-Before we could return
and capture the strangers,

326
00:12:19,107 --> 00:12:21,142
the Volkites appeared
and destroyed them

327
00:12:21,175 --> 00:12:22,577
with their atom guns.

328
00:12:22,610 --> 00:12:24,145
-I wanted them alive!

329
00:12:24,178 --> 00:12:25,678
-They are alive.

330
00:12:25,712 --> 00:12:27,247
-Well then everybody's happy.

331
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:28,682
-Well then, shall we eat?

332
00:12:28,715 --> 00:12:29,750
There's Dutch loaf.

333
00:12:29,783 --> 00:12:31,685
-Sharad's men have captured
them, Your Majesty,

334
00:12:31,718 --> 00:12:33,553
and they're heading
for the Sacred City!

335
00:12:33,587 --> 00:12:34,755
-Enough TV for you two.

336
00:12:34,788 --> 00:12:35,857
Now get outside and play!

337
00:12:35,890 --> 00:12:37,892
-Recapture the
prisoners at any cost.

338
00:12:37,925 --> 00:12:38,292
You

339
00:12:38,325 --> 00:12:38,925
-Hi-keeba.

340
00:12:43,163 --> 00:12:45,098
-Oh! [IMITATES SOUND
OF HEADS BUMPING ROCK]

341
00:12:45,132 --> 00:12:46,067
-Owie!

342
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:46,767
-Oh, my head.

343
00:12:46,833 --> 00:12:47,333
-My head.

344
00:12:47,367 --> 00:12:49,068
-Oh, watch out in back!

345
00:12:49,102 --> 00:12:51,338
-Ow!

346
00:12:51,372 --> 00:12:52,673
[Hum along with
background music]

347
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:01,915
-Oh, look.

348
00:13:01,948 --> 00:13:02,817
It's the three horsemen--
no, the four horsemen-- no,

349
00:13:02,850 --> 00:13:05,018
the several horsemen
of the apocalypse.

350
00:13:07,755 --> 00:13:09,357
-Oh, Mother Superior
jumped the gun.

351
00:13:09,390 --> 00:13:09,990
-Nope.

352
00:13:14,728 --> 00:13:15,662
-Whooaaa!

353
00:13:15,695 --> 00:13:18,865
Big hill, everybody!

354
00:13:18,898 --> 00:13:20,667
-Well, what do you know?

355
00:13:20,667 --> 00:13:27,107
[Theme music]

356
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:28,342
-Ooh.

357
00:13:28,375 --> 00:13:30,343
-The Dick Clark story on ABC.

358
00:13:34,248 --> 00:13:36,283
-He any relationship
to Dick Cheney?

359
00:13:36,317 --> 00:13:37,785
-I don't think so.

360
00:13:37,818 --> 00:13:39,018
-Oh.

361
00:13:39,052 --> 00:13:40,988
-Marion?

362
00:13:41,022 --> 00:13:46,860
-TVs Marion from Happy Days.

363
00:13:46,893 --> 00:13:49,897
-It sounds like the
soundtrack is drunk.

364
00:13:49,930 --> 00:13:56,837
[Exaggeratedly imitates music]

365
00:13:56,870 --> 00:14:00,875
-Oh, Ted Fleshman, Jr., John
Russell, Jr., Lon Chaney, Jr.,

366
00:14:00,908 --> 00:14:03,377
Sammy Davis, Jr., Junior
Samples, Junior Absorbing, Jr.

367
00:14:03,410 --> 00:14:04,945
-Lots of juniors.

368
00:14:09,350 --> 00:14:11,718
-(TOGETHER) Vy
Russell in flight.

369
00:14:11,752 --> 00:14:14,455
[BUZZES] Afternoon
delight-- aww.

370
00:14:14,488 --> 00:14:15,990
-It's cute, huh?

371
00:14:16,023 --> 00:14:17,725
-Pollexfen, for
your "denfentures".

372
00:14:17,758 --> 00:14:18,292
-Den-then-ten-tures.

373
00:14:22,362 --> 00:14:23,763
-All of justice?

374
00:14:23,797 --> 00:14:25,765
That's kind of a gross
exaggeration, isn't it?

375
00:14:25,798 --> 00:14:26,733
-It's not a court of justice.

376
00:14:26,767 --> 00:14:27,702
It's a court of law.

377
00:14:30,403 --> 00:14:32,840
-Who is Detective Division?

378
00:14:32,873 --> 00:14:33,907
-Hmm.

379
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:35,475
-I'm Lieutenant Dick Chasen.

380
00:14:35,508 --> 00:14:36,277
-He's eating breakfast.

381
00:14:36,310 --> 00:14:39,247
-This hadn't been
a routine case.

382
00:14:39,280 --> 00:14:41,948
And to find the answer, I've had
to put a lot of little pieces

383
00:14:41,982 --> 00:14:42,650
together.

384
00:14:42,683 --> 00:14:43,983
-It's parents' day at Attica!

385
00:14:44,017 --> 00:14:46,887
--most cases can
be called closed.

386
00:14:46,920 --> 00:14:49,490
It was the day before the
Butcher was to be executed.

387
00:14:49,523 --> 00:14:51,525
-He was visited by his attorney
Paul Lowe in the death-house.

388
00:14:51,558 --> 00:14:52,258
-San Quentin Crisp.

389
00:14:52,292 --> 00:14:54,227
Cute.

390
00:14:54,262 --> 00:14:56,230
-It was at this first
meeting the Butcher Benton

391
00:14:56,263 --> 00:14:57,898
case took its first switch.

392
00:14:57,932 --> 00:15:00,100
-Jimmy Smits.

393
00:15:00,133 --> 00:15:01,468
-One last tip, Butcher.

394
00:15:01,502 --> 00:15:03,403
The evidence against
you is so strong,

395
00:15:03,437 --> 00:15:04,938
the government turned
down your appeal.

396
00:15:04,972 --> 00:15:06,273
-Aww, they still
think your father's

397
00:15:06,307 --> 00:15:07,908
more talented than you are.

398
00:15:07,942 --> 00:15:09,377
-You started rough,
and now you're

399
00:15:09,410 --> 00:15:11,612
still trying to throw me curves.

400
00:15:11,645 --> 00:15:13,113
-Look, I don't blame
you for being edgy,

401
00:15:13,147 --> 00:15:14,482
but get this straight.

402
00:15:14,515 --> 00:15:16,483
I didn't double cross you.

403
00:15:16,517 --> 00:15:18,185
I never worked
harder for a client.

404
00:15:18,218 --> 00:15:19,987
-You mean you never
worked harder for a client

405
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:20,553
to get him sentenced.

406
00:15:20,587 --> 00:15:20,988
-Oh.

407
00:15:21,022 --> 00:15:22,155
-Oh, that hurt.

408
00:15:22,188 --> 00:15:24,223
-You're a fool, Butcher.

409
00:15:24,257 --> 00:15:26,893
If you hadn't tried to double
cross Squeamy Ellis and Joe

410
00:15:26,927 --> 00:15:28,528
Marcellia, they wouldn't
have turned state's evidence

411
00:15:28,562 --> 00:15:29,228
against you.

412
00:15:29,262 --> 00:15:29,897
-Touche.

413
00:15:29,930 --> 00:15:30,998
-But you had to get greedy.

414
00:15:31,032 --> 00:15:34,100
You wanted to keep the
whole $600,000 for yourself.

415
00:15:34,133 --> 00:15:35,068
-Which one's in jail?

416
00:15:35,102 --> 00:15:36,003
-Hmm.

417
00:15:36,037 --> 00:15:37,303
-Boys got sore, and
I don't blame them.

418
00:15:37,370 --> 00:15:38,672
-It was all your idea.

419
00:15:38,705 --> 00:15:40,507
You planned the whole job.

420
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:41,908
You hired us.

421
00:15:41,942 --> 00:15:43,410
When you found out
I'd staked the money,

422
00:15:43,443 --> 00:15:45,678
you decided it was
time for me to die.

423
00:15:45,712 --> 00:15:46,880
-Shh, Mom's here.

424
00:15:46,913 --> 00:15:49,983
-You got those two crumbs to
turn state's evidence on me.

425
00:15:50,017 --> 00:15:52,285
You stinking rotten mouthpiece.

426
00:15:52,318 --> 00:15:55,555
-That's Mr. Stinking
Rotten Mouthpiece to you.

427
00:15:55,588 --> 00:15:58,658
-Now look, what's the sense
in not giving me the money?

428
00:15:58,692 --> 00:16:00,060
It's not gonna do you any good.

429
00:16:00,093 --> 00:16:00,460
-Oh yeah.

430
00:16:00,493 --> 00:16:01,028
Yeah.

431
00:16:01,062 --> 00:16:02,228
-It's locked up in a CD.

432
00:16:02,262 --> 00:16:04,498
-I'll have the satisfaction
of knowing that none of you

433
00:16:04,532 --> 00:16:06,367
three crumbs are gonna spend it.

434
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:07,600
-What about Eva?

435
00:16:07,633 --> 00:16:09,937
-Don't you owe her something?

436
00:16:09,970 --> 00:16:11,372
You tell me where
the money is, I'll

437
00:16:11,405 --> 00:16:13,373
see that she gets your share.

438
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:15,742
-I've got a different idea.

439
00:16:15,775 --> 00:16:19,078
I'm going to kill you
and Squeamy and Joe.

440
00:16:19,112 --> 00:16:20,680
Then I'll take
care of Eva myself.

441
00:16:20,713 --> 00:16:21,748
-Neat.

442
00:16:21,782 --> 00:16:25,152
-You thick-headed ape,
you're gonna die tomorrow.

443
00:16:25,185 --> 00:16:25,985
-Oh, that's right.

444
00:16:26,020 --> 00:16:28,255
I stand corrected.

445
00:16:28,288 --> 00:16:31,058
-Remember what I said.

446
00:16:31,092 --> 00:16:31,692
I'm gonna getcha.

447
00:16:31,725 --> 00:16:32,192
-Sucker.

448
00:16:32,225 --> 00:16:32,893
-All three of ya.

449
00:16:32,927 --> 00:16:35,562
-Wait, there's only one of me.

450
00:16:35,595 --> 00:16:39,032
-Even for you, Butcher,
that'd be quite a trick.

451
00:16:39,065 --> 00:16:41,235
So long, dead man.

452
00:16:41,268 --> 00:16:43,237
-OK, see you late--
[SPUTTERS] Hey!

453
00:16:43,270 --> 00:16:45,472
-Remember what I said.

454
00:16:45,505 --> 00:16:46,773
I'm gonna kill you.

455
00:16:46,807 --> 00:16:48,542
All three of you.

456
00:16:48,575 --> 00:16:51,578
-The butcher, the baker,
and the candlestick maker.

457
00:16:51,612 --> 00:16:53,780
-The thief, the
cook, the wife, and--

458
00:16:53,813 --> 00:16:55,348
-Whoa-ho, Centerville.

459
00:16:55,382 --> 00:16:58,618
Real great place to
bring your kids up.

460
00:17:02,655 --> 00:17:03,690
-French dips.

461
00:17:03,723 --> 00:17:06,193
-Back in Los Angeles, two
men sat in a bar room,

462
00:17:06,227 --> 00:17:08,495
listening to a newscaster
tell his headline story.

463
00:17:08,528 --> 00:17:09,830
-Oh, I've heard this joke.

464
00:17:09,863 --> 00:17:10,830
-The Butcher's
threatened to kill

465
00:17:10,863 --> 00:17:11,698
Paul Lowe, Squeamy
Ellis, and Joe Marcellia.

466
00:17:11,732 --> 00:17:12,365
-Listerine?

467
00:17:12,398 --> 00:17:14,435
[Exaggerated chewing sounds]

468
00:17:14,468 --> 00:17:17,503
-For them having turned
state's evidence against him.

469
00:17:17,537 --> 00:17:21,175
-Squeamy Ellis didn't seem
happy over the Butcher's threat.

470
00:17:21,208 --> 00:17:23,377
-(MOCKING) They're not happy
with the Butcher's threat!

471
00:17:23,410 --> 00:17:26,713
-But they had figured, once a
dead man, always a dead man.

472
00:17:26,747 --> 00:17:27,213
-That's smart.

473
00:17:27,247 --> 00:17:28,248
-Like a boy scout.

474
00:17:28,282 --> 00:17:29,483
-Ooh!

475
00:17:29,517 --> 00:17:31,618
-This was one time everyone was
in for a surprise, including

476
00:17:31,652 --> 00:17:33,320
the entire police department.

477
00:17:33,353 --> 00:17:35,688
-Mhm, mhm.

478
00:17:35,722 --> 00:17:37,157
Go on.

479
00:17:37,190 --> 00:17:38,358
Come on.

480
00:17:38,392 --> 00:17:41,128
-Hmm, wonder what Billy's
doing in Family Circus today.

481
00:17:41,162 --> 00:17:41,828
-Wow.

482
00:17:41,862 --> 00:17:43,630
Prime cuts go on sale at noon.

483
00:17:43,663 --> 00:17:45,198
-Building code under fire.

484
00:17:49,503 --> 00:17:50,837
-[GASPS] Oh.

485
00:17:50,870 --> 00:17:53,407
-You see the headlines, Captain?

486
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:55,542
-Teletype just came
in from San Francisco.

487
00:17:55,575 --> 00:17:56,943
-Here's the receipt
for your paper.

488
00:17:56,977 --> 00:17:59,713
-Set for 5 o'clock
this afternoon.

489
00:17:59,747 --> 00:18:02,448
And like the newspapers
say, he still

490
00:18:02,482 --> 00:18:04,283
refuses to talk about
that stolen money.

491
00:18:04,317 --> 00:18:06,253
-(MUTTERED) Still refuses to
talk about that stolen money.

492
00:18:06,287 --> 00:18:08,388
-Well, I still have hopes
of coming up with something.

493
00:18:08,422 --> 00:18:09,857
-Ooh, he sat on the pen set!

494
00:18:09,890 --> 00:18:12,292
-That's what I
dropped by for, Dick.

495
00:18:12,325 --> 00:18:14,495
Butcher's execution, the
department's marking his case

496
00:18:14,528 --> 00:18:15,195
closed.

497
00:18:15,228 --> 00:18:17,297
That means you'll be reassigned.

498
00:18:17,330 --> 00:18:18,365
-Hmm.

499
00:18:18,398 --> 00:18:21,335
-I spent a whole year
trying to break this case.

500
00:18:21,368 --> 00:18:22,870
As far as I'm concerned,
Butcher's death

501
00:18:22,903 --> 00:18:24,403
is just the beginning.

502
00:18:24,437 --> 00:18:25,972
-Could you reassign me
with Pepper Anderson?

503
00:18:26,005 --> 00:18:29,810
-I can't prove it, but I think
that Paul Lowe was the top man.

504
00:18:29,843 --> 00:18:33,347
And even though I have to keep
working on it while off duty,

505
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:36,350
I'm going to get Lowe, Squeamy
Ellis, and Joe Marcellia.

506
00:18:36,383 --> 00:18:36,783
-Hmm.

507
00:18:36,817 --> 00:18:37,650
-OK, Lieutenant.

508
00:18:37,683 --> 00:18:38,852
-Hey, hey, hey!

509
00:18:38,885 --> 00:18:40,420
-Officially, I've
taken you off the case.

510
00:18:40,453 --> 00:18:41,120
-Touch, touch.

511
00:18:41,155 --> 00:18:42,022
-Unofficially, I wish you luck.

512
00:18:42,055 --> 00:18:44,023
-Hey, you forgot to
pat his head, too.

513
00:18:44,057 --> 00:18:44,825
-Do me a favor.

514
00:18:44,858 --> 00:18:46,025
Keep me posted.

515
00:18:46,058 --> 00:18:46,727
-Will do.

516
00:18:46,760 --> 00:18:47,693
-Wait, isn't this your office?

517
00:18:47,727 --> 00:18:48,395
-Thanks, John.

518
00:18:51,765 --> 00:18:53,533
I headed for Eva Martin.

519
00:18:53,567 --> 00:18:54,935
-But then, who didn't? [PANTS]

520
00:18:54,968 --> 00:18:57,270
-She worked in the
local burlesque house.

521
00:18:57,303 --> 00:19:00,607
I'm a cop, and my job is to
ask questions and get answers.

522
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:01,675
-All right, talk!

523
00:19:01,708 --> 00:19:04,578
-So I decided I might as
well start again with her.

524
00:19:04,612 --> 00:19:05,945
-Da-dum, da-dum, da-dum.

525
00:19:05,978 --> 00:19:08,682
-And I walked
really, really fast.

526
00:19:08,715 --> 00:19:09,315
Morning!

527
00:19:12,085 --> 00:19:14,320
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) Either
those curtains go, or I do.

528
00:19:14,353 --> 00:19:16,088
-Come in.

529
00:19:16,122 --> 00:19:17,023
-Oh, hi.

530
00:19:17,057 --> 00:19:18,025
I saw your sister outside.

531
00:19:18,058 --> 00:19:20,527
You know, the quiet one?

532
00:19:20,560 --> 00:19:21,495
-What do you want?

533
00:19:21,528 --> 00:19:23,030
-I'd like to talk to you.

534
00:19:23,063 --> 00:19:24,565
-Talk?

535
00:19:24,598 --> 00:19:26,065
You're a cop, aren't you?

536
00:19:26,098 --> 00:19:27,800
-That's a good question.

537
00:19:27,833 --> 00:19:29,703
I can't even find a
three-ton armored car,

538
00:19:29,737 --> 00:19:31,772
let alone the $600,000.

539
00:19:31,805 --> 00:19:33,607
I've been on the case a year.

540
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:36,510
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
Well you're a crappy cop.

541
00:19:36,510 --> 00:19:38,612
Look, it's 9:00 AM and
I'm dressed like this.

542
00:19:38,645 --> 00:19:39,478
Leave me alone!
-Look, I told you.

543
00:19:39,512 --> 00:19:40,747
I don't know anything
about the money.

544
00:19:40,780 --> 00:19:41,682
I never did.

545
00:19:41,715 --> 00:19:43,150
-Benton's gonna be
dead in a little while.

546
00:19:43,183 --> 00:19:44,952
I thought it might
make a difference.

547
00:19:44,985 --> 00:19:47,620
-I never knew anything
about the money!

548
00:19:47,653 --> 00:19:48,588
-Money, you know?

549
00:19:48,622 --> 00:19:51,123
Medium exchange,
storehouse of value, green.

550
00:19:51,157 --> 00:19:54,995
-I didn't even know Charles was
in the rackets until the trial.

551
00:19:55,028 --> 00:19:56,930
-Now take it easy, kid.

552
00:19:56,963 --> 00:19:58,465
-You're on, Eva!

553
00:19:58,498 --> 00:19:59,933
-Hey, it's Al Jolson.

554
00:19:59,967 --> 00:20:02,802
-What's he doing out there?

555
00:20:02,835 --> 00:20:04,705
-So, uh, can I wear
your clothes now?

556
00:20:07,873 --> 00:20:12,845
-Pff, boy, that burlesque cow
sure puts on a good brunch.

557
00:20:12,878 --> 00:20:14,615
-It's a Jack Ruby planet.

558
00:20:14,648 --> 00:20:18,018
-The Jack Ruby program!

559
00:20:18,052 --> 00:20:19,920
-Oh, damn.

560
00:20:19,953 --> 00:20:22,188
It's my evil twin.

561
00:20:22,222 --> 00:20:23,757
-Well, let's see here.

562
00:20:23,790 --> 00:20:26,460
GWM seeks leatherman for-- oh.

563
00:20:29,597 --> 00:20:31,498
-He's wearing espadrilles.

564
00:20:31,532 --> 00:20:32,933
Oh, even criminals
have to punch in.

565
00:20:32,967 --> 00:20:35,635
What do you know?

566
00:20:35,668 --> 00:20:37,570
-Ha, now I can drink.

567
00:20:37,603 --> 00:20:40,640
-Honey, here's your wrap.

568
00:20:40,673 --> 00:20:42,075
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
Thanks, Mr. Lithgow.

569
00:20:42,108 --> 00:20:44,712
-Anytime.

570
00:20:44,745 --> 00:20:46,847
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
New stuff killed-- oh, hi.

571
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:56,657
-Oh, mommy.

572
00:20:56,690 --> 00:20:58,792
-It's his mother?

573
00:20:58,825 --> 00:20:59,860
-What a dump.

574
00:20:59,893 --> 00:21:01,160
Who said that?

575
00:21:01,193 --> 00:21:02,963
-Two months ago, Butcher
Benton, without revealing

576
00:21:02,997 --> 00:21:06,300
the whereabouts of the $600,000
stolen in the armored car

577
00:21:06,333 --> 00:21:08,835
hold-up, paid for his
crimes against society

578
00:21:08,868 --> 00:21:10,637
in the gas chamber
at San Quentin.

579
00:21:10,670 --> 00:21:13,140
-Walter Winchell is
hiding behind the curtain.

580
00:21:13,173 --> 00:21:14,007
-There.

581
00:21:14,040 --> 00:21:15,908
Jeez, I hate Howard Stern.

582
00:21:15,942 --> 00:21:17,243
-Come on, baby.

583
00:21:17,277 --> 00:21:19,680
Don't waste tears on Benton.

584
00:21:19,713 --> 00:21:22,582
-I feel so badly about Charles.

585
00:21:22,615 --> 00:21:26,353
If I'd known, maybe I
could've done something.

586
00:21:26,387 --> 00:21:27,220
-How about a drink?

587
00:21:27,253 --> 00:21:29,188
We could both use it.

588
00:21:29,222 --> 00:21:31,692
-An artist can't drink
while she's working.

589
00:21:31,725 --> 00:21:32,958
-Then how about a karab shake?

590
00:21:38,198 --> 00:21:42,102
-Well, I've got blanch
peanuts here, if-- hmm.

591
00:21:44,972 --> 00:21:46,807
-It's from Ed McMahon.

592
00:21:46,840 --> 00:21:47,973
-Well now what's the matter?

593
00:21:48,008 --> 00:21:50,043
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
They spelled my name wrong.

594
00:21:50,077 --> 00:21:52,112
-It's a message from Charles.

595
00:21:52,145 --> 00:21:56,315
He told me to open
it if-- he died.

596
00:21:56,350 --> 00:21:56,750
-[Whimpers]

597
00:21:56,783 --> 00:21:58,818
-Finale, Eva.

598
00:21:58,852 --> 00:21:59,853
-All right.

599
00:21:59,887 --> 00:22:01,988
-Jolson again.

600
00:22:02,022 --> 00:22:04,692
-How about a steak
after the show?

601
00:22:04,725 --> 00:22:08,395
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
Oh, he always offers me beef.

602
00:22:08,428 --> 00:22:10,997
-Dumb, you stupid
dumb-- the idiot!

603
00:22:11,030 --> 00:22:14,100
What is with me today?

604
00:22:14,133 --> 00:22:15,735
-Hmm.

605
00:22:15,768 --> 00:22:18,205
Well it says here, "Dear Eva,
don't eat too much steak.

606
00:22:18,238 --> 00:22:19,005
Love, Charles."

607
00:22:19,038 --> 00:22:20,007
-Hmm.

608
00:22:20,040 --> 00:22:22,275
-Opening the letter the
Butcher are given Eva,

609
00:22:22,308 --> 00:22:24,912
found the map outlining a
section of the city sewer

610
00:22:24,945 --> 00:22:25,845
system.

611
00:22:25,878 --> 00:22:26,747
-It's his varicose veins.

612
00:22:26,780 --> 00:22:27,747
-Clearly identified
was the spot where

613
00:22:27,747 --> 00:22:30,750
the Butcher had hidden
the stolen payroll.

614
00:22:30,783 --> 00:22:32,285
He replaced it with a $50 bill.

615
00:22:32,318 --> 00:22:33,287
-And a pork chop.

616
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:37,157
-I guess he figured $50
to gain $600,000 was not

617
00:22:37,190 --> 00:22:38,425
a bad day's work.

618
00:22:38,458 --> 00:22:40,327
-No one will be seated during
the frightening letter-folding

619
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:40,960
scene.

620
00:22:40,993 --> 00:22:42,228
-Put it in a mutual fund.

621
00:22:45,798 --> 00:22:53,507
[Doors closing]

622
00:22:53,540 --> 00:22:57,010
-Welcome to the Satellite
of Love's annual Any Excuse

623
00:22:57,043 --> 00:22:58,512
for a Parade parade.

624
00:22:58,545 --> 00:23:01,213
This year, we salute
The Undersea Kingdom.

625
00:23:01,247 --> 00:23:03,350
I'm your host, Alan
Thicke, and my co-host

626
00:23:03,383 --> 00:23:05,852
down on the parade
route is Mary Frann.

627
00:23:05,885 --> 00:23:06,687
-Mary Frann?

628
00:23:06,720 --> 00:23:08,122
You said I could
be Dick Van Patten.

629
00:23:08,155 --> 00:23:09,388
-Oh, sure thing, Dick.

630
00:23:09,422 --> 00:23:11,825
-OK, our first float
is turning the corner

631
00:23:11,858 --> 00:23:14,160
onto Gypsy Rose Lane.

632
00:23:14,193 --> 00:23:16,930
Gypsy, as you know, is
this year's grand marshal.

633
00:23:16,963 --> 00:23:18,298
-Always an honor!

634
00:23:18,332 --> 00:23:21,233
-This float is a tribute
to Ray "Crash" Corrigan.

635
00:23:21,267 --> 00:23:23,370
-And it's a good thing
Crash isn't driving.

636
00:23:23,403 --> 00:23:24,805
-Ho ho, that's right, Dick.

637
00:23:24,838 --> 00:23:26,440
Hey, look, there's little Billy!

638
00:23:26,473 --> 00:23:28,308
-Hurry, Diane.

639
00:23:28,342 --> 00:23:31,410
-That float is sponsored by
the American Dairy Association

640
00:23:31,445 --> 00:23:33,980
and is made entirely of
recycled milk cartons.

641
00:23:34,013 --> 00:23:36,148
-Dairy, you don't
find it underwater,

642
00:23:36,182 --> 00:23:37,450
but it's got water in it.

643
00:23:37,450 --> 00:23:41,120
The ADA inviting you to enjoy
dairy in all its permutations

644
00:23:41,153 --> 00:23:42,922
at least three times a day.

645
00:23:42,955 --> 00:23:44,892
-Here comes the
Florida Institute

646
00:23:44,925 --> 00:23:48,093
of Marine Biology marching
band, playing the popular love

647
00:23:48,128 --> 00:23:50,030
theme from The Undersea Kingdom.

648
00:23:50,063 --> 00:23:52,365
[Garbled music]

649
00:23:52,398 --> 00:23:57,103
-And here's a perennial
favorite, the lovable Tom Servo

650
00:23:57,137 --> 00:23:59,605
from TV's Mystery
Science Theater 3,000.

651
00:23:59,638 --> 00:24:02,275
He portrays Dr.
Norton's submarine from?

652
00:24:02,308 --> 00:24:03,210
-The Undersea Kingdom!

653
00:24:03,243 --> 00:24:03,977
-That's right.

654
00:24:04,010 --> 00:24:05,212
-Did you know, Alan?

655
00:24:05,245 --> 00:24:07,480
It took 18 seamstresses working
round the clock three months

656
00:24:07,513 --> 00:24:09,348
to bring the plucky bot to life.

657
00:24:09,382 --> 00:24:10,550
-In his balloon!

658
00:24:10,617 --> 00:24:11,818
-Mhm.

659
00:24:11,818 --> 00:24:15,288
-And Tom is filled with over
15,000 cubic yards of hydrogen.

660
00:24:15,322 --> 00:24:16,490
-I think you mean helium, Dick.

661
00:24:16,523 --> 00:24:19,258
-Following Servo is the American
Pepper Association float.

662
00:24:19,292 --> 00:24:21,093
Hats off to pepper.

663
00:24:21,127 --> 00:24:21,962
-Uh, pepper.

664
00:24:21,995 --> 00:24:23,697
Up your day with pepper!

665
00:24:23,730 --> 00:24:25,365
-The American Pepper
Association invites

666
00:24:25,398 --> 00:24:27,067
you to try pepper
as a side dish.

667
00:24:27,100 --> 00:24:29,135
-You know, pepper was used
in the miniature sequences

668
00:24:29,168 --> 00:24:32,305
in The Undersea Kingdom to
simulate the ocean floor.

669
00:24:32,338 --> 00:24:33,507
-But pepper.

670
00:24:33,540 --> 00:24:34,340
[Inhaling rapidly]

671
00:24:34,373 --> 00:24:35,342
-Look out!

672
00:24:35,375 --> 00:24:36,677
[Screams]

673
00:24:36,710 --> 00:24:38,645
-Achoo!

674
00:24:38,678 --> 00:24:40,213
-(DEADPAN) Oh, no.

675
00:24:40,247 --> 00:24:42,348
Servo has burst into flames.

676
00:24:42,382 --> 00:24:43,583
People are running around.

677
00:24:43,650 --> 00:24:44,150
Fire is shooting.

678
00:24:44,183 --> 00:24:45,552
Oh, oh, the humanity.

679
00:24:45,585 --> 00:24:47,753
Uh, people are
running and screaming.

680
00:24:47,787 --> 00:24:50,190
Uh, we've got commercial
sign, and I'm just

681
00:24:50,223 --> 00:24:52,458
gonna step into
this doorway here.

682
00:24:52,492 --> 00:24:58,398
[Theme music]

683
00:24:58,432 --> 00:24:59,265
-Two hundred miles--

684
00:24:59,298 --> 00:25:00,233
-[coughing]

685
00:25:00,267 --> 00:25:02,368
-San Francisco, the case
took another switch.

686
00:25:02,402 --> 00:25:02,802
Which--

687
00:25:02,835 --> 00:25:04,037
-Jimmy Smits.

688
00:25:04,070 --> 00:25:06,105
--turned the next 72 hours into
one long, hideous nightmare.

689
00:25:06,138 --> 00:25:07,607
-My in-laws came to visit.

690
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:08,708
-[Guffaws]

691
00:25:08,742 --> 00:25:11,210
-Biochemist was
making preparations

692
00:25:11,243 --> 00:25:13,213
for a final experiment
which he hoped

693
00:25:13,247 --> 00:25:16,182
would lead the way
to a cure for cancer.

694
00:25:16,215 --> 00:25:18,452
He'd been successful
with laboratory animals.

695
00:25:18,485 --> 00:25:19,385
-Well, who hasn't?

696
00:25:19,418 --> 00:25:21,520
-The last step called
for a human body.

697
00:25:21,555 --> 00:25:22,488
-That's where I came in.

698
00:25:22,522 --> 00:25:24,323
-Bradshaw's assistant
quickly made a deal

699
00:25:24,357 --> 00:25:26,793
with the local mortuary and
returned to the laboratory

700
00:25:26,827 --> 00:25:28,728
with the dead body
of Butcher Benton.

701
00:25:28,762 --> 00:25:31,765
-Then he hired the winner of
the Joe Flynn look-alike contest

702
00:25:31,798 --> 00:25:33,267
to come on down.

703
00:25:33,300 --> 00:25:35,102
-(GERMAN ACCENT) S'all
right under the sheet?

704
00:25:35,135 --> 00:25:38,238
S'all right.

705
00:25:38,272 --> 00:25:38,872
-You made good time.

706
00:25:38,905 --> 00:25:40,473
-Well, I didn't waste any.

707
00:25:40,507 --> 00:25:41,240
-Any trouble?

708
00:25:41,273 --> 00:25:42,342
-McHale?

709
00:25:42,375 --> 00:25:43,277
-No, I just handed the money
over and moved the body

710
00:25:43,310 --> 00:25:45,812
into your station wagon,
and that was that.

711
00:25:45,845 --> 00:25:46,512
-Good.

712
00:25:46,545 --> 00:25:47,413
-(NASALLY VOICE) This is fun.

713
00:25:52,752 --> 00:25:53,787
-Checking him for change.

714
00:25:53,820 --> 00:25:54,853
-He's in good shape.

715
00:25:54,887 --> 00:25:55,722
-Well, I hope so.

716
00:25:55,755 --> 00:25:57,090
-For a dead man.

717
00:25:59,692 --> 00:26:01,293
-We'll get other
blood samples ready,

718
00:26:01,327 --> 00:26:02,595
then we'll run this
one through fast.

719
00:26:02,628 --> 00:26:03,430
-Right.

720
00:26:03,463 --> 00:26:07,433
-But, uh, first
get junior samples.

721
00:26:07,467 --> 00:26:09,702
-Now wheel him into the
machine and then raise him.

722
00:26:09,735 --> 00:26:11,672
-Raise with a good
Midwestern value.

723
00:26:11,705 --> 00:26:14,440
Loyalty, hard work, you know.

724
00:26:14,473 --> 00:26:16,643
-A little late for a CAT scan.

725
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:24,483
-We will sell no
wine before its time.

726
00:26:24,517 --> 00:26:26,485
-(BABY TALK) Ooh, he's a
hungry little fella, there.

727
00:26:26,518 --> 00:26:27,887
Isn't he a little hungry fella?

728
00:26:32,425 --> 00:26:32,825
-McHale?

729
00:26:36,630 --> 00:26:37,563
-Elvis!

730
00:26:37,597 --> 00:26:38,632
-Ready?

731
00:26:38,665 --> 00:26:41,702
-You mean you're going to
give him 287,000 volts?

732
00:26:41,735 --> 00:26:43,202
-Yes, yes.

733
00:26:43,235 --> 00:26:44,703
-OK, you're the boss.

734
00:26:44,737 --> 00:26:47,373
-We'll check for that
before we start dissecting.

735
00:26:47,407 --> 00:26:49,408
Turn off the lights.

736
00:26:49,442 --> 00:26:53,245
-We need a dramatic effect.

737
00:26:53,278 --> 00:26:55,515
-He just invented
the Lite-Brite!

738
00:26:55,548 --> 00:26:56,650
-Hmm.

739
00:26:56,683 --> 00:26:57,317
-Ribs!

740
00:26:57,350 --> 00:26:58,718
That's what I'm hungry for!

741
00:26:58,752 --> 00:26:59,452
-Mmm.

742
00:26:59,485 --> 00:27:01,988
-Old boy, if you
respond properly

743
00:27:02,022 --> 00:27:06,525
and my theory is sound, you'll
be more famous dead than alive.

744
00:27:06,558 --> 00:27:08,762
-Like most artists.

745
00:27:08,795 --> 00:27:11,463
-Jimmy Smits.

746
00:27:11,497 --> 00:27:16,002
[Electric buzzing]

747
00:27:16,035 --> 00:27:16,635
-It's alive!

748
00:27:16,670 --> 00:27:17,037
It's--

749
00:27:17,070 --> 00:27:17,637
-No.

750
00:27:17,670 --> 00:27:18,037
-Oh.

751
00:27:18,070 --> 00:27:20,740
Aw.

752
00:27:20,773 --> 00:27:22,308
-Bond meter.

753
00:27:22,342 --> 00:27:23,643
-Oh.

754
00:27:23,677 --> 00:27:28,515
-(SINGING) GE, we bring
Lon Chaney to life!

755
00:27:28,548 --> 00:27:29,882
-Inspector Henderson
and Captain Binghamton,

756
00:27:29,915 --> 00:27:31,918
working together
for your future!

757
00:27:31,952 --> 00:27:33,420
-I think it needs
the electric light

758
00:27:33,487 --> 00:27:35,422
orchestra to bring
it all together.

759
00:27:35,455 --> 00:27:37,857
-El Dorado, El
Dorado, El Dorado.

760
00:27:40,727 --> 00:27:42,595
-You see, it goes
all the way to 11.

761
00:27:48,668 --> 00:27:51,738
-Oh, gee, get a garbage
bag and a squeegee.

762
00:27:51,772 --> 00:27:52,738
--rotation of the part.

763
00:27:57,010 --> 00:27:58,512
-(SINGING) Old man, liver.

764
00:27:58,545 --> 00:28:01,480
-Mr. Bradshaw, he's
beginning to breathe.

765
00:28:01,513 --> 00:28:03,082
You've brought this
man back to life!

766
00:28:03,115 --> 00:28:04,717
-With Coast!

767
00:28:04,750 --> 00:28:06,352
-The heart muscle
simply responded

768
00:28:06,385 --> 00:28:08,655
to a terrific electrical
shock, that's all.

769
00:28:08,655 --> 00:28:10,557
-Really?

770
00:28:10,590 --> 00:28:13,727
-Yes, but it does prove that
the cells still function.

771
00:28:13,760 --> 00:28:14,927
-So, lunch?

772
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:16,628
-Dr. Bradshaw--

773
00:28:16,662 --> 00:28:17,463
-Heal the child within!

774
00:28:17,497 --> 00:28:18,865
-He is alive.

775
00:28:18,898 --> 00:28:21,433
-The shock reaction,
it can't last.

776
00:28:21,467 --> 00:28:25,105
-His cells are multiplying
now, if your theory is right.

777
00:28:25,138 --> 00:28:30,643
-If we could keep that
reaction going longer-- but

778
00:28:30,677 --> 00:28:33,413
it's worth a try.

779
00:28:33,447 --> 00:28:35,448
-Get the body out.

780
00:28:35,482 --> 00:28:37,650
-How come I always have
to get the body out?

781
00:28:41,822 --> 00:28:43,022
-Coffee?

782
00:28:43,088 --> 00:28:45,158
-Come on, get up
for school, faker.

783
00:28:45,192 --> 00:28:46,025
Come on, up and at 'em.

784
00:28:46,058 --> 00:28:46,625
Rise and shine.

785
00:28:46,658 --> 00:28:47,593
Come on.

786
00:28:51,663 --> 00:28:55,502
-Oh, jeez, I've seen calf
liver cuter than him.

787
00:28:55,535 --> 00:28:57,470
-Get some adrenaline
from the supply room.

788
00:28:57,503 --> 00:28:58,672
Some anti-nitrite too.

789
00:28:58,705 --> 00:28:59,905
Hurry, hurry!

790
00:28:59,938 --> 00:29:01,808
-100% pure adrenaline.

791
00:29:01,842 --> 00:29:05,178
-(WHISPERED) Point break.

792
00:29:05,212 --> 00:29:07,047
-Oh, boy.

793
00:29:07,080 --> 00:29:10,183
Did I get electrocuted
last night?

794
00:29:10,217 --> 00:29:17,690
[Wheezing sound effect]

795
00:29:17,723 --> 00:29:19,025
-Senor Wences lives!

796
00:29:23,663 --> 00:29:25,665
-[GROANS] What am I smelling?

797
00:29:25,698 --> 00:29:28,033
Did somebody die in
here?-- oh, I did!

798
00:29:31,137 --> 00:29:32,138
-Uh, oh.

799
00:29:32,172 --> 00:29:35,275
Oh, did I-- did I go
home with you last night?

800
00:29:35,308 --> 00:29:36,943
Oh.

801
00:29:36,977 --> 00:29:38,277
-Can I have a glass of water?

802
00:29:38,310 --> 00:29:41,013
I'm really parched.

803
00:29:41,047 --> 00:29:41,982
-[Grunts]

804
00:29:42,015 --> 00:29:44,550
-Hey, now.

805
00:29:44,583 --> 00:29:45,918
-[Grunts]

806
00:29:45,952 --> 00:29:47,887
-Your heart can't
stand the strain.

807
00:29:47,920 --> 00:29:49,222
-[Grunts]

808
00:29:49,255 --> 00:29:50,790
-Now you must lie still.

809
00:29:50,823 --> 00:29:53,192
Please, please.

810
00:29:53,225 --> 00:29:54,793
-Look, my back
teeth are floating.

811
00:29:54,827 --> 00:29:57,897
I've got to go to the bathroom.

812
00:29:57,930 --> 00:30:00,267
-He's got a saggy
diaper that leaks.

813
00:30:00,300 --> 00:30:02,302
-Yuck.

814
00:30:02,335 --> 00:30:06,005
-You're right, I
had that coming.

815
00:30:06,038 --> 00:30:08,842
-Uh, you think people
come back from the dead?

816
00:30:08,875 --> 00:30:10,910
You should see this lab
around quitting time.

817
00:30:10,943 --> 00:30:12,712
[Guffawing]

818
00:30:12,745 --> 00:30:14,213
-Back to the Butcher.

819
00:30:14,247 --> 00:30:16,917
He tried to talk, but
the electrical voltage

820
00:30:16,950 --> 00:30:18,685
had burned out his vocal cords.

821
00:30:18,718 --> 00:30:19,852
-Thanks for everything, bye now.

822
00:30:19,885 --> 00:30:21,922
-Hadn't destroyed his brain.

823
00:30:21,955 --> 00:30:23,322
He knew who he was.

824
00:30:23,355 --> 00:30:26,292
He knew who he hated.

825
00:30:26,325 --> 00:30:27,327
-I'm in here!

826
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:29,928
-Hey, use the damn stairs!

827
00:30:29,962 --> 00:30:33,032
-Kill Lowe, Squeamy,
and Joe Marcellia.

828
00:30:33,065 --> 00:30:33,767
-Hey!

829
00:30:33,800 --> 00:30:35,033
Wait-- oh.

830
00:30:35,067 --> 00:30:36,768
-The electrical voltage that Dr.
Bradshaw had given the Butcher

831
00:30:36,802 --> 00:30:38,738
had increased his
cellular structure

832
00:30:38,772 --> 00:30:40,740
to the point where he
was no longer a man.

833
00:30:40,773 --> 00:30:42,408
-But made for a woman.

834
00:30:42,442 --> 00:30:43,677
-Dr. Bradshaw's
experiment had created

835
00:30:43,710 --> 00:30:47,280
a vicious, brutal animal with
an almost inconceivable amount

836
00:30:47,313 --> 00:30:48,180
of strength.

837
00:30:48,213 --> 00:30:49,415
-So he joined the LA
police department.

838
00:30:49,448 --> 00:30:52,052
-Dr. Bradshaw must have believed
the Butcher's regained life

839
00:30:52,085 --> 00:30:53,987
would last for
only a few minutes.

840
00:30:54,020 --> 00:30:54,920
-If refrigerated.

841
00:30:54,953 --> 00:30:56,855
-Therefore, instead
of calling for help,

842
00:30:56,890 --> 00:31:00,125
the doctor decided to
handle his creation alone.

843
00:31:00,192 --> 00:31:01,793
-Now, easy Benton.

844
00:31:01,827 --> 00:31:02,362
Just relax.

845
00:31:02,395 --> 00:31:04,063
-You relax, I'm dead.

846
00:31:04,097 --> 00:31:05,998
--doctor, and I want
to try to help you.

847
00:31:06,032 --> 00:31:07,233
Do you understand me?

848
00:31:07,267 --> 00:31:09,002
You're in my laboratory.

849
00:31:09,035 --> 00:31:11,370
-You're Milton,
my brand new son.

850
00:31:11,403 --> 00:31:12,305
-You were declared dead.

851
00:31:12,338 --> 00:31:13,573
-Hmm.

852
00:31:13,607 --> 00:31:15,173
-I tried it an experiment on you
and brought you back to life,

853
00:31:15,207 --> 00:31:16,508
although I didn't intend that.

854
00:31:16,542 --> 00:31:17,210
-Uh-huh.

855
00:31:17,243 --> 00:31:17,877
-Now, you've had a great shock.

856
00:31:17,910 --> 00:31:20,447
Now, now come and sit down.

857
00:31:20,480 --> 00:31:21,813
Be quiet, now.

858
00:31:21,847 --> 00:31:22,615
Come and sit down.

859
00:31:22,648 --> 00:31:24,217
-C'mere, I'll fix
you a nice Coke.

860
00:31:24,250 --> 00:31:27,453
-Be quiet, he tells him?

861
00:31:27,487 --> 00:31:28,053
-McHale?

862
00:31:28,087 --> 00:31:29,222
Oh.

863
00:31:29,255 --> 00:31:33,425
I can't wait until
I'm in the boat next.

864
00:31:33,458 --> 00:31:34,060
-Careful.

865
00:31:34,127 --> 00:31:35,328
His reactions are violent.

866
00:31:35,362 --> 00:31:36,462
-How do you explain this?

867
00:31:36,495 --> 00:31:37,797
-Ah, his reactions are violent.

868
00:31:37,830 --> 00:31:40,433
-100 times, perhaps thousands.

869
00:31:40,467 --> 00:31:41,533
His strength is unbelievable.

870
00:31:41,567 --> 00:31:42,835
-Hm.

871
00:31:42,868 --> 00:31:43,770
Gum?

872
00:31:43,803 --> 00:31:45,405
-No, it's too late
for the ammo nitrate.

873
00:31:45,438 --> 00:31:47,340
I want to get some
blood samples,

874
00:31:47,373 --> 00:31:49,408
make some tests on him,
find out what's happened.

875
00:31:49,442 --> 00:31:51,043
Now get me a syringe.

876
00:31:51,077 --> 00:31:51,978
-Yes, sir!

877
00:31:55,080 --> 00:31:56,482
-Good, now get me a sarong.

878
00:32:02,255 --> 00:32:04,923
-Hm?

879
00:32:04,957 --> 00:32:06,525
-Relax, the first one's free.

880
00:32:06,558 --> 00:32:07,927
-What the--?

881
00:32:07,993 --> 00:32:09,028
Oh, I get it.

882
00:32:09,062 --> 00:32:10,630
He's indestructible.

883
00:32:10,663 --> 00:32:12,032
-(IN UNISON) Ohhh.

884
00:32:12,065 --> 00:32:14,467
-You bent my needle,
you freaked out maniac!

885
00:32:14,500 --> 00:32:17,537
-Why won't the needle
penetrate the skin?

886
00:32:17,570 --> 00:32:21,640
-The tissue must be nearly
a solid mass of cells.

887
00:32:21,673 --> 00:32:23,075
-How're we gonna get
the blood samples?

888
00:32:23,108 --> 00:32:24,443
-Molting is very glamorous.

889
00:32:24,477 --> 00:32:26,578
-Surgically, perhaps.

890
00:32:26,612 --> 00:32:27,280
-Surgically?

891
00:32:27,313 --> 00:32:31,350
Wait, I want a second opinion.

892
00:32:31,383 --> 00:32:32,252
-Take it easy.

893
00:32:32,285 --> 00:32:33,887
You can't leave here now.

894
00:32:33,920 --> 00:32:35,922
I'm responsible for
your being alive.

895
00:32:35,955 --> 00:32:36,588
[Strangling sounds]

896
00:32:36,622 --> 00:32:37,657
-You're not the boss of me.

897
00:32:37,690 --> 00:32:40,092
[Chuckles]

898
00:32:40,125 --> 00:32:45,432
-You know, I really cannot
tell which is more fun to kill.

899
00:32:45,465 --> 00:32:46,498
Huh, it's a draw.

900
00:32:50,703 --> 00:32:52,172
-Huh.

901
00:32:52,205 --> 00:32:53,605
Well, done and done.

902
00:32:53,638 --> 00:32:55,173
Killed two birds with one stone.

903
00:32:58,243 --> 00:33:00,647
Sorry, guys.

904
00:33:00,680 --> 00:33:03,315
Uh, say, you know if there's
a breakfast place near-- oh,

905
00:33:03,348 --> 00:33:04,117
that's right.

906
00:33:04,150 --> 00:33:04,550
You're dead.

907
00:33:04,583 --> 00:33:05,952
Sorry, I forgot.

908
00:33:05,985 --> 00:33:07,953
-(SINGING) This will
be the day that I die.

909
00:33:07,987 --> 00:33:10,188
(SPEAKING) I finally understand
the lyrics to that "American

910
00:33:10,222 --> 00:33:12,325
Pie." (RESUMES SINGING)
Drove my Chevy--

911
00:33:12,358 --> 00:33:19,732
[theme music]

912
00:33:19,765 --> 00:33:22,735
-Well, he's wandered
into a Mr. Bulky's.

913
00:33:22,768 --> 00:33:23,737
-Oh, let's see here.

914
00:33:23,770 --> 00:33:27,240
Licorice whips, Jujubes,
Slo-Pokes, Lon chaney,

915
00:33:27,273 --> 00:33:28,040
Junior Mints.

916
00:33:28,073 --> 00:33:28,640
[Chuckles]

917
00:33:28,673 --> 00:33:31,510
-Uh, hello?

918
00:33:31,543 --> 00:33:32,212
Anybody work here?

919
00:33:32,245 --> 00:33:35,280
Can I get some service, please?

920
00:33:35,313 --> 00:33:37,583
-Towards the end of this
case, Captain Lauder and I

921
00:33:37,617 --> 00:33:41,020
found out the doctor and his
assistant had been murdered.

922
00:33:41,053 --> 00:33:43,222
But I am getting
ahead of my story.

923
00:33:43,255 --> 00:33:44,323
-I'll say.

924
00:33:44,357 --> 00:33:46,025
-The Butcher didn't know
how he had been brought back

925
00:33:46,058 --> 00:33:47,527
from the dead, and
he didn't care.

926
00:33:47,560 --> 00:33:48,560
-Oh, he's under the bleachers.

927
00:33:48,593 --> 00:33:50,530
-All he knew-- he was alive.

928
00:33:50,530 --> 00:33:51,363
-Kinda.

929
00:33:51,397 --> 00:33:52,165
A little.

930
00:33:52,198 --> 00:33:54,167
-And this
monster-made-man started

931
00:33:54,200 --> 00:33:56,635
thinking about Los Angeles.

932
00:33:56,668 --> 00:34:00,105
-"The Eyes of Laura Mars."

933
00:34:00,138 --> 00:34:02,475
Oh, I feel better now.

934
00:34:02,508 --> 00:34:05,210
Say, this whole coming
back from the dead thing

935
00:34:05,243 --> 00:34:06,812
isn't so bad after all!

936
00:34:06,845 --> 00:34:07,447
Huh!

937
00:34:07,480 --> 00:34:08,782
Now I gotta tap a kidney.

938
00:34:08,815 --> 00:34:11,550
Be right back.

939
00:34:11,583 --> 00:34:12,718
-On that night in Los Angeles--

940
00:34:12,752 --> 00:34:13,920
-Taquitos!

941
00:34:13,953 --> 00:34:16,622
-I decided to stop playing
detective for a little while.

942
00:34:16,655 --> 00:34:17,490
-Dude looks like a lady, huh?

943
00:34:17,523 --> 00:34:18,858
-I went to the
burlesque house to ask

944
00:34:18,892 --> 00:34:20,325
Eva to have dinner with me.

945
00:34:20,358 --> 00:34:21,493
-I think I'm going
to like it here.

946
00:34:21,527 --> 00:34:24,397
-After all, the Butcher was
dead, and if I got lucky,

947
00:34:24,430 --> 00:34:26,365
sooner or later I
figured Paul Lowe--

948
00:34:26,398 --> 00:34:27,533
[creaking sound effects]

949
00:34:27,567 --> 00:34:28,700
-Would lead me to
the stolen payroll.

950
00:34:28,733 --> 00:34:30,703
In a way, that possibility--

951
00:34:30,737 --> 00:34:34,507
-I want everybody on stage
for the big Whoville number!

952
00:34:34,540 --> 00:34:35,808
-Want some?

953
00:34:35,842 --> 00:34:38,243
-It's not fair to watch
the show from backstage.

954
00:34:38,277 --> 00:34:39,878
-Next time, I'll pay admission.

955
00:34:39,912 --> 00:34:40,913
-[Makes_rimshot_noise]

956
00:34:40,947 --> 00:34:43,548
-I take it by that remark
you like my routine.

957
00:34:43,582 --> 00:34:44,583
-Yeah, you're pretty good.

958
00:34:44,617 --> 00:34:45,518
-For a guy.

959
00:34:45,552 --> 00:34:47,420
-There's something,
like, kind of spooky

960
00:34:47,453 --> 00:34:49,522
about watching Casey
Adams flirt, isn't there?

961
00:34:49,555 --> 00:34:50,255
-Uh-huh.

962
00:34:50,288 --> 00:34:51,757
-It shouldn't be
hard for someone

963
00:34:51,790 --> 00:34:54,627
like you to get a
job in a class spot.

964
00:34:54,660 --> 00:34:56,362
-If you weren't a
detective, I'd be

965
00:34:56,395 --> 00:34:57,363
willing to bet with
a line like that,

966
00:34:57,397 --> 00:35:00,332
your next remark would
be, "How about dinner?"

967
00:35:00,365 --> 00:35:01,600
[Makes rimshot noise]

968
00:35:01,633 --> 00:35:04,370
-Well, not exactly dinner, but a
hamburger might be a good idea.

969
00:35:04,403 --> 00:35:05,572
-Ahh, meat again?

970
00:35:05,605 --> 00:35:09,208
-If you're asking in an official
capacity, I can't say no.

971
00:35:09,242 --> 00:35:09,942
-She's got a limo.

972
00:35:09,975 --> 00:35:11,443
-I'm off duty.

973
00:35:11,477 --> 00:35:13,445
-For the past six months,
all I've ever called you

974
00:35:13,478 --> 00:35:14,780
is Lieutenant Casey.

975
00:35:14,813 --> 00:35:16,482
Do you have a first name?

976
00:35:16,515 --> 00:35:17,450
-Mhm.

977
00:35:17,483 --> 00:35:17,883
Dick.

978
00:35:17,917 --> 00:35:20,820
-That figures.

979
00:35:20,853 --> 00:35:23,923
-Give me 10 minutes for a
quick change, because you just

980
00:35:23,957 --> 00:35:26,925
made yourself a date,
Lieutenant-- I mean, Dick.

981
00:35:26,958 --> 00:35:27,993
-All right.

982
00:35:28,027 --> 00:35:30,328
[Purrs]

983
00:35:30,362 --> 00:35:31,897
-So why'd they go to Expo 67?

984
00:35:31,963 --> 00:35:33,532
-No, that's Brazil 76.

985
00:35:33,565 --> 00:35:34,333
-I see.

986
00:35:34,367 --> 00:35:36,935
-Well, the guy
spares no expense.

987
00:35:36,968 --> 00:35:39,738
-I haven't had hamburger in
the backseat of an automobile

988
00:35:39,772 --> 00:35:41,007
with a guy in a lot of years.

989
00:35:41,040 --> 00:35:42,608
-(IMITATES FEMALE VOICE)
You're a cheap jerk.

990
00:35:42,642 --> 00:35:45,545
-From now on, we'll
do it more often.

991
00:35:45,578 --> 00:35:47,447
-Fine.

992
00:35:47,480 --> 00:35:50,883
I always figured a policeman
wasn't really a human being.

993
00:35:50,917 --> 00:35:53,485
I mean, unless he was
arresting someone or trying

994
00:35:53,518 --> 00:35:55,320
to solve a case, he was unhappy.

995
00:35:55,353 --> 00:35:55,888
-That's nice.

996
00:35:55,922 --> 00:35:57,088
Pass me the jack sauce.

997
00:35:57,122 --> 00:35:59,792
-In fact, everyone on the
force gets fed up occasionally

998
00:35:59,825 --> 00:36:01,460
with the job they do.

999
00:36:01,493 --> 00:36:02,995
-That's why we beat
up Rodney King.

1000
00:36:03,028 --> 00:36:05,497
-Maybe we're more
human than anyone else.

1001
00:36:05,532 --> 00:36:06,665
-Huh!

1002
00:36:06,698 --> 00:36:09,902
-You see so much trouble,
you begin to hate the world.

1003
00:36:09,935 --> 00:36:12,003
But as long as the world's
filled with people,

1004
00:36:12,037 --> 00:36:14,573
there have to be men
who enforce the laws.

1005
00:36:14,607 --> 00:36:16,742
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
Um, can you take me home now?

1006
00:36:16,775 --> 00:36:18,077
-What made you join
the department?

1007
00:36:18,110 --> 00:36:19,778
-Now who's making
with the questions?

1008
00:36:19,812 --> 00:36:20,647
-[Chuckles_sarcastically]

1009
00:36:20,680 --> 00:36:21,547
-No, honest.

1010
00:36:21,580 --> 00:36:22,815
I'd love to know.

1011
00:36:22,848 --> 00:36:25,785
-Well, it's really
a very short story.

1012
00:36:25,818 --> 00:36:26,852
-By Chekhov.

1013
00:36:26,885 --> 00:36:29,355
-When I got out of the Air
Corps, I knocked around.

1014
00:36:29,388 --> 00:36:31,957
-[Sighs_loudly]

1015
00:36:31,990 --> 00:36:34,427
-Then I went to work
for an oil company.

1016
00:36:34,460 --> 00:36:35,427
-That's why I'm so greasy.

1017
00:36:35,460 --> 00:36:38,430
-One day, I went back to
college on the GI bill.

1018
00:36:38,463 --> 00:36:39,132
Just before graduation--

1019
00:36:39,165 --> 00:36:40,132
-[hums_impatiently]

1020
00:36:40,165 --> 00:36:42,568
-I noticed all about the
police department exams.

1021
00:36:42,602 --> 00:36:44,937
-[Yawns_loudly]

1022
00:36:44,970 --> 00:36:49,875
-[Exaggerated_snoring_sounds]

1023
00:36:49,908 --> 00:36:51,943
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) Can
I throw up out the window?

1024
00:36:51,977 --> 00:36:52,745
-I have one more question.

1025
00:36:52,778 --> 00:36:54,480
Will you give me
a straight answer?

1026
00:36:54,513 --> 00:36:55,848
-Try me.

1027
00:36:55,882 --> 00:36:59,418
-Am I still part of your
Charles Benton investigation?

1028
00:36:59,452 --> 00:37:00,152
-And how.

1029
00:37:00,185 --> 00:37:02,555
-I mean, right now.

1030
00:37:02,588 --> 00:37:03,822
-I don't think so.

1031
00:37:03,855 --> 00:37:05,090
-(MOCKINGLY, IN UNISON)
I don't think so.

1032
00:37:05,123 --> 00:37:06,792
-As far as the
department's concerned,

1033
00:37:06,825 --> 00:37:11,197
when Benton was executed,
the case was marked closed.

1034
00:37:11,230 --> 00:37:13,498
If it's on to me,
that's not good enough.

1035
00:37:13,532 --> 00:37:14,800
-[Exaggerated_yawn]

1036
00:37:14,833 --> 00:37:16,068
-I don't follow.

1037
00:37:16,102 --> 00:37:18,972
-Well, the $60,000
is still missing.

1038
00:37:19,005 --> 00:37:21,773
A fast-talking underworld
lawyer like Paul Lowe

1039
00:37:21,807 --> 00:37:24,210
is walking around a free man.

1040
00:37:24,243 --> 00:37:24,710
-Better?

1041
00:37:24,743 --> 00:37:25,612
-Sure, I'm better.

1042
00:37:25,645 --> 00:37:27,045
-Someday, no matter
how long it takes,

1043
00:37:27,078 --> 00:37:28,948
I'll get him and the money.

1044
00:37:28,982 --> 00:37:31,750
Until I do, the Benton case
for me it's not closed.

1045
00:37:31,783 --> 00:37:32,718
-Oh, bravo.

1046
00:37:32,752 --> 00:37:33,285
-Yaaaay.

1047
00:37:33,318 --> 00:37:34,087
[Clapping and whistling]

1048
00:37:34,120 --> 00:37:35,253
-Bravo.

1049
00:37:35,287 --> 00:37:37,255
-Charles Benton never told
me anything about the money.

1050
00:37:37,290 --> 00:37:39,558
-Well, I've been sure
of that for a long time.

1051
00:37:39,592 --> 00:37:41,593
-Yeah.

1052
00:37:41,627 --> 00:37:43,628
-But how did you
ever get mixed up

1053
00:37:43,662 --> 00:37:44,697
with anyone like the Butcher?

1054
00:37:44,730 --> 00:37:46,265
-(IMITATES FEMALE VOICE)
Uh, well, I wanted a good,

1055
00:37:46,298 --> 00:37:47,132
lean brisket, and--

1056
00:37:47,165 --> 00:37:49,202
-It just happened.

1057
00:37:49,268 --> 00:37:52,538
I had a roommate who used
to date Benton all the time.

1058
00:37:52,572 --> 00:37:55,540
One day, she packed
and left for Chicago.

1059
00:37:55,573 --> 00:37:56,742
Benton carried
the torch for her,

1060
00:37:56,775 --> 00:37:58,177
and he used my
shoulder to cry on.

1061
00:37:58,210 --> 00:37:59,778
-Then he burned down the house.

1062
00:37:59,812 --> 00:38:01,547
-Next thing I knew, we were
having dinner together.

1063
00:38:01,580 --> 00:38:03,748
And a few weeks
later, I was labeled

1064
00:38:03,782 --> 00:38:07,820
by the police as this number
one suspect and Benton's girl.

1065
00:38:07,853 --> 00:38:09,155
But it was never like that!

1066
00:38:09,188 --> 00:38:10,022
(YAWNING) Uh-huh, good.

1067
00:38:10,055 --> 00:38:11,290
-I made only one mistake.

1068
00:38:11,323 --> 00:38:12,792
-Excuse me, guys.

1069
00:38:12,825 --> 00:38:13,658
-[Stretching_sounds]

1070
00:38:13,692 --> 00:38:14,227
-You mean--

1071
00:38:14,260 --> 00:38:15,293
-It's a real yawn fest.

1072
00:38:15,327 --> 00:38:17,563
-Arrested for the robbery,
you had no idea how--

1073
00:38:17,597 --> 00:38:18,563
-Hey, hey, hey.

1074
00:38:18,597 --> 00:38:19,732
-Huh?

1075
00:38:19,765 --> 00:38:21,633
Oh, oh sorry, I just thought
I'd stretch out a little.

1076
00:38:21,700 --> 00:38:23,735
-I want to know what you've
got going on over here.

1077
00:38:23,768 --> 00:38:24,937
-I was just sort of looking.

1078
00:38:24,970 --> 00:38:26,638
-And all we ever talked about
was Mags, my old roommate.

1079
00:38:26,672 --> 00:38:27,707
-No, go.

1080
00:38:27,740 --> 00:38:30,342
-Last time I saw him, he
tried to get too friendly,

1081
00:38:30,375 --> 00:38:32,978
and that's when I told
him he had the wrong girl.

1082
00:38:33,012 --> 00:38:33,845
-Then I had him electrocuted.

1083
00:38:33,878 --> 00:38:37,617
-Like I said, I already
made my mistake.

1084
00:38:37,650 --> 00:38:39,618
-What about the burlesque house?

1085
00:38:39,652 --> 00:38:40,753
-Can we go back there?

1086
00:38:40,787 --> 00:38:44,057
-Please, please, when is
this scene gonna end, please?

1087
00:38:44,090 --> 00:38:47,225
I'm getting really tired and
cranky, and-- I don't know.

1088
00:38:47,258 --> 00:38:48,327
-And I won a beauty contest.

1089
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:48,893
-No!

1090
00:38:48,927 --> 00:38:50,028
Not her life's story!

1091
00:38:50,062 --> 00:38:51,097
-No, please.

1092
00:38:51,130 --> 00:38:52,297
Please get rid of it.

1093
00:38:52,330 --> 00:38:53,698
Will you please?

1094
00:38:53,732 --> 00:38:54,767
-After which?

1095
00:38:54,800 --> 00:38:55,300
-Turn it off!

1096
00:38:55,333 --> 00:38:56,368
Turn it off!

1097
00:38:56,368 --> 00:38:57,870
-Really.

1098
00:38:57,870 --> 00:39:00,405
-Come on.

1099
00:39:00,438 --> 00:39:02,073
-And then one day,
I read an ad about--

1100
00:39:02,107 --> 00:39:03,975
-Hey, have you no
sense of decency?

1101
00:39:04,008 --> 00:39:05,912
Could you please end this scene?

1102
00:39:05,945 --> 00:39:07,012
-Yeah, jeez.

1103
00:39:07,045 --> 00:39:08,180
-And you know something?

1104
00:39:08,213 --> 00:39:09,882
It's like any other job.

1105
00:39:09,915 --> 00:39:10,682
-Yeah, where you strip.

1106
00:39:10,717 --> 00:39:12,183
-If you're looking
for trouble, you

1107
00:39:12,217 --> 00:39:13,718
can find it even
in the school room.

1108
00:39:13,752 --> 00:39:14,787
-Oh boy, oh boy.

1109
00:39:14,820 --> 00:39:15,722
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
That's where I hang out.

1110
00:39:15,755 --> 00:39:16,588
-Oh boy, oh boy, oh
boy oh boy, oh boy.

1111
00:39:16,622 --> 00:39:17,222
-When the screen
test didn't work out,

1112
00:39:17,255 --> 00:39:19,458
why didn't you go on home?

1113
00:39:19,492 --> 00:39:22,360
-Why don't you go on home
and stop it! [VOICE_BREAKS]

1114
00:39:22,395 --> 00:39:23,162
-Joel, it's OK.

1115
00:39:23,195 --> 00:39:24,463
It's OK, honey.

1116
00:39:24,497 --> 00:39:24,963
It's OK.

1117
00:39:24,997 --> 00:39:27,232
It's OK, I'm here.

1118
00:39:27,265 --> 00:39:30,135
-I don't know about you,
but I do eight shows a day,

1119
00:39:30,202 --> 00:39:31,837
and at this hour, I'm bushed.

1120
00:39:31,870 --> 00:39:32,905
-So do I.

1121
00:39:32,938 --> 00:39:33,505
-Oh, I see.

1122
00:39:33,538 --> 00:39:34,507
The big brush-off, eh?

1123
00:39:34,540 --> 00:39:35,842
-Mind if I drive you home?

1124
00:39:35,875 --> 00:39:36,542
-No.

1125
00:39:36,575 --> 00:39:38,177
-Mighty Casey has struck out!

1126
00:39:38,210 --> 00:39:39,912
-And I'll tell you
something else.

1127
00:39:39,945 --> 00:39:40,847
-(IN UNISON) Noooo!

1128
00:39:40,880 --> 00:39:42,448
-I haven't minded
anything about tonight.

1129
00:39:42,482 --> 00:39:44,015
Not even the hamburgers.

1130
00:39:44,048 --> 00:39:45,383
-[Insincere_chuckling]

1131
00:39:45,417 --> 00:39:47,920
-Kill them!

1132
00:39:47,953 --> 00:39:49,288
-I kept playing tag with Lowe.

1133
00:39:49,322 --> 00:39:51,523
He made four telephone calls.

1134
00:39:51,557 --> 00:39:53,125
Three of them
didn't interest me.

1135
00:39:53,158 --> 00:39:54,393
But one did.

1136
00:39:54,427 --> 00:39:54,793
The one--

1137
00:39:54,827 --> 00:39:55,293
-Break, no!

1138
00:39:55,327 --> 00:39:56,162
-To Joe Marcellia.

1139
00:39:56,195 --> 00:39:56,895
-Go!

1140
00:39:56,928 --> 00:39:57,328
Go go go!

1141
00:39:57,362 --> 00:39:58,230
OK, go go!

1142
00:39:58,263 --> 00:39:58,830
Go, go!

1143
00:39:58,863 --> 00:40:00,065
Go!

1144
00:40:00,098 --> 00:40:01,267
-For some unknown reason, I
began to get the feeling that--

1145
00:40:01,300 --> 00:40:01,700
-Go, go!

1146
00:40:01,733 --> 00:40:02,802
Go go go, go!

1147
00:40:02,835 --> 00:40:04,503
-This case was finally about
to explode in my favor.

1148
00:40:04,537 --> 00:40:07,573
-Hey, I'm walking here.

1149
00:40:07,607 --> 00:40:09,108
-[Laughing]

1150
00:40:09,142 --> 00:40:09,975
-Ah!

1151
00:40:10,008 --> 00:40:12,945
-Aunt Bee, after dark.

1152
00:40:12,978 --> 00:40:14,247
-Well look at that!

1153
00:40:14,280 --> 00:40:16,448
I've been staggering
on two legs,

1154
00:40:16,482 --> 00:40:18,583
but he's doing it on four!

1155
00:40:18,617 --> 00:40:20,085
-Try that, you flea bag!

1156
00:40:20,118 --> 00:40:21,853
-Well look who's calling names.

1157
00:40:21,887 --> 00:40:23,588
At least I ain't a squealer!

1158
00:40:23,622 --> 00:40:24,557
A stoolie!

1159
00:40:24,590 --> 00:40:25,623
-[Yells]

1160
00:40:25,657 --> 00:40:27,158
-It's David Mamet's
annotation of the Lockhorns!

1161
00:40:27,192 --> 00:40:28,227
-All right, Harry,
that's enough.

1162
00:40:28,260 --> 00:40:29,195
I'll take care of them.

1163
00:40:29,228 --> 00:40:30,162
I wanna talk to them.

1164
00:40:30,195 --> 00:40:31,263
-OK, Mr. Lowe.

1165
00:40:31,297 --> 00:40:32,598
But any future business
you've got with this psycho,

1166
00:40:32,632 --> 00:40:34,400
take care of it
upstairs in your office.

1167
00:40:34,433 --> 00:40:36,235
He keeps showing around
here, sooner or later,

1168
00:40:36,268 --> 00:40:37,870
he's gonna get slugged--
cripple or no cripple.

1169
00:40:37,903 --> 00:40:39,205
-Vic Tayback, ladies
and gentlemen.

1170
00:40:39,238 --> 00:40:43,142
[Clapping and cheering]

1171
00:40:43,175 --> 00:40:45,343
-Ew, she's covered
with dead things.

1172
00:40:45,377 --> 00:40:46,578
-Yuck.

1173
00:40:46,612 --> 00:40:47,480
-Go away.

1174
00:40:47,513 --> 00:40:48,580
-Let me get this straight.

1175
00:40:48,613 --> 00:40:52,183
So every table has a bottle
of Old Smuggler on it?

1176
00:40:52,217 --> 00:40:53,052
-No.

1177
00:40:53,085 --> 00:40:54,887
-I need us sharp.

1178
00:40:54,920 --> 00:40:57,388
You'll get one,
after I talk to you.

1179
00:40:57,422 --> 00:40:58,457
You sober enough to understand?

1180
00:40:58,490 --> 00:40:59,358
-[Hiccups]

1181
00:40:59,392 --> 00:41:00,292
-Yeah.

1182
00:41:00,325 --> 00:41:01,260
I ran out of dough.

1183
00:41:01,293 --> 00:41:02,327
-I was going to make cookies!

1184
00:41:02,360 --> 00:41:05,197
-Look, you used to be the
best torchman in town.

1185
00:41:05,230 --> 00:41:05,998
-Well, thank you.

1186
00:41:06,032 --> 00:41:06,798
-Now I don't think
you could crack

1187
00:41:06,832 --> 00:41:08,533
a safe if you knew
the combination.

1188
00:41:08,567 --> 00:41:09,100
-No jobs.

1189
00:41:09,133 --> 00:41:10,168
-Torchmen crack safes?

1190
00:41:10,202 --> 00:41:12,237
-Nobody wants me since I
turned state's on Benton.

1191
00:41:12,270 --> 00:41:13,072
That's your doing.

1192
00:41:13,138 --> 00:41:13,972
You told me.

1193
00:41:14,005 --> 00:41:15,273
-All right, quit crying.

1194
00:41:15,307 --> 00:41:15,842
-Baby.

1195
00:41:15,875 --> 00:41:17,275
-I've got a job for you.

1196
00:41:17,308 --> 00:41:19,212
If you can stay off
that stuff long enough--

1197
00:41:19,245 --> 00:41:20,545
-You know I can
if I've got a job.

1198
00:41:20,578 --> 00:41:20,947
How much?

1199
00:41:20,980 --> 00:41:21,580
What is it?

1200
00:41:21,613 --> 00:41:23,382
-Pays five bottles a week.

1201
00:41:23,415 --> 00:41:24,650
-Is two grand enough for ya?

1202
00:41:24,683 --> 00:41:25,517
-Where?

1203
00:41:25,550 --> 00:41:27,318
-Right there where
we pulled the job.

1204
00:41:27,352 --> 00:41:29,255
-Say your leg stopped dead.

1205
00:41:29,288 --> 00:41:32,157
-Two grand ain't
much, out of $600,000.

1206
00:41:32,190 --> 00:41:33,025
-The other expenses.

1207
00:41:33,058 --> 00:41:34,193
Besides, that money's
plenty enough.

1208
00:41:34,227 --> 00:41:36,528
-Got kind of a Bruce Willis
smirk, there, doesn't he?

1209
00:41:36,562 --> 00:41:37,062
-Now do you want it?

1210
00:41:37,095 --> 00:41:38,097
Or do I get another?

1211
00:41:38,130 --> 00:41:38,997
-I'll do it.

1212
00:41:39,030 --> 00:41:40,465
How soon do you want
me to go to work?

1213
00:41:40,498 --> 00:41:41,100
-Duh--

1214
00:41:41,133 --> 00:41:42,067
-Set it up tomorrow night.

1215
00:41:42,100 --> 00:41:42,902
-Let's us go.

1216
00:41:42,935 --> 00:41:44,437
-It's like a
criminal temp agency.

1217
00:41:47,238 --> 00:41:48,040
-One.

1218
00:41:48,107 --> 00:41:50,075
And that's all till
the job's over.

1219
00:41:50,108 --> 00:41:53,045
-Or he broke his thumb.

1220
00:41:53,078 --> 00:41:58,750
[Theme music]

1221
00:41:58,783 --> 00:42:02,353
-OK, and your hand burns,
sending a single to your brain

1222
00:42:02,387 --> 00:42:05,090
to tell you to move your
hand off the electric heater.

1223
00:42:05,123 --> 00:42:06,592
-Oh, so in a way, pain is good.

1224
00:42:06,625 --> 00:42:09,093
Otherwise, uh, we might
be hurt and not know it,

1225
00:42:09,127 --> 00:42:10,295
and die during social studies.

1226
00:42:10,328 --> 00:42:10,997
-Exactly.

1227
00:42:11,030 --> 00:42:12,430
It's all part of a bigger plan.

1228
00:42:12,463 --> 00:42:13,365
Get it, Crow?

1229
00:42:13,398 --> 00:42:14,400
-Yeah, I guess.

1230
00:42:14,433 --> 00:42:15,667
-Ok.

1231
00:42:15,700 --> 00:42:18,203
Everybody knows there's no way a
person could be indestructible.

1232
00:42:18,237 --> 00:42:20,438
But just for fun, what would you
do if you were indestructible?

1233
00:42:20,472 --> 00:42:23,775
-Uhh, I'd, uh, hammer
nails with my skull!

1234
00:42:23,808 --> 00:42:24,810
No, no wait!

1235
00:42:24,843 --> 00:42:26,545
I'd be a high performance
drill bit, or, uh--

1236
00:42:26,578 --> 00:42:28,747
-I'd jump in a stump
grinder to amuse my friends.

1237
00:42:28,780 --> 00:42:29,348
-Really?

1238
00:42:29,382 --> 00:42:30,348
-Oh, sure!

1239
00:42:30,382 --> 00:42:32,652
Better yet, I'd rent 15
acres of wooded farmland

1240
00:42:32,685 --> 00:42:35,187
and invite people to hunt
me down-- for a small fee.

1241
00:42:35,220 --> 00:42:36,288
-Oh, good one, Tommy.

1242
00:42:36,322 --> 00:42:37,422
How about this?

1243
00:42:37,455 --> 00:42:40,225
Um, I'd invite college students
to staple announcements

1244
00:42:40,258 --> 00:42:42,795
and posters on me,
and let them open

1245
00:42:42,828 --> 00:42:43,928
beer bottles with
my eye sockets.

1246
00:42:43,962 --> 00:42:46,198
-Oh, then I'd allow myself
to be used as an ashtray!

1247
00:42:46,232 --> 00:42:47,265
-Oh yeah?

1248
00:42:47,298 --> 00:42:49,802
Well, I'd wear a wool
sweater without a t-shirt.

1249
00:42:49,835 --> 00:42:51,237
-Huh.

1250
00:42:51,270 --> 00:42:52,672
-It's Peruvian.

1251
00:42:52,705 --> 00:42:54,105
Real itchy, scratchy.

1252
00:42:54,138 --> 00:42:56,508
-But you get used
to it after a while.

1253
00:42:56,542 --> 00:42:57,342
-Well listen, Crow.

1254
00:42:57,408 --> 00:42:59,845
Every indestructible
fantasy does not

1255
00:42:59,878 --> 00:43:01,313
have to be gruesome or horrible.

1256
00:43:01,347 --> 00:43:02,280
-Yes it does.

1257
00:43:02,313 --> 00:43:03,448
-Sure.

1258
00:43:03,482 --> 00:43:04,248
-Uh, OK.

1259
00:43:04,283 --> 00:43:05,050
Well how about this?

1260
00:43:05,083 --> 00:43:06,518
I go down to the
corner every day,

1261
00:43:06,552 --> 00:43:08,487
and immolate myself
for money, kind of

1262
00:43:08,520 --> 00:43:09,622
like a street performer.

1263
00:43:09,655 --> 00:43:10,022
-Yeah!

1264
00:43:10,055 --> 00:43:10,722
-Yeah, yeah!

1265
00:43:10,755 --> 00:43:12,190
Now you're cooking with Crisco!

1266
00:43:12,223 --> 00:43:12,692
Crisco.

1267
00:43:12,725 --> 00:43:13,558
Hey, I know!

1268
00:43:13,592 --> 00:43:15,927
I'd bob for doughnuts
in a deep fryer!

1269
00:43:15,962 --> 00:43:16,895
-I've got one.

1270
00:43:16,928 --> 00:43:18,463
I'd date Sean Young
and then cheat on her

1271
00:43:18,497 --> 00:43:19,398
with Roseanne Barr.

1272
00:43:19,432 --> 00:43:21,233
-Oh, that's kid stuff
compared to this.

1273
00:43:21,267 --> 00:43:22,233
Now get this.

1274
00:43:22,267 --> 00:43:23,902
I'd march right into the
Department of Justice

1275
00:43:23,935 --> 00:43:25,370
and take the Warren
Commission file.

1276
00:43:25,403 --> 00:43:26,372
-Ooh.

1277
00:43:26,405 --> 00:43:27,940
Well, I'd go to Rome and
unveil the conspiracy

1278
00:43:27,973 --> 00:43:29,273
between the mob and the Vatican.

1279
00:43:29,342 --> 00:43:30,475
-Oh yeah?

1280
00:43:30,542 --> 00:43:32,812
Well, I'd wear, uh, white
tennis shoes with black socks,

1281
00:43:32,845 --> 00:43:34,613
and just wait for
kids to beat me up.

1282
00:43:34,647 --> 00:43:35,947
[Alarm blares]

1283
00:43:35,982 --> 00:43:37,515
-Oh my god, movie!

1284
00:43:37,548 --> 00:43:37,950
-Whoa.

1285
00:43:37,983 --> 00:43:45,357
[Doors creak]

1286
00:43:45,390 --> 00:43:48,560
-Ah, well.

1287
00:43:48,593 --> 00:43:50,495
-Meanwhile, in
giant gila monster.

1288
00:43:50,528 --> 00:43:52,965
-I gotta take a big
indestructible wiz.

1289
00:43:52,998 --> 00:43:54,467
-Oh, that's tasteless.

1290
00:43:54,500 --> 00:43:55,533
-Sorry.

1291
00:43:55,567 --> 00:43:58,803
-I may be indestructible,
but I still hurt inside!

1292
00:43:58,837 --> 00:44:00,005
-[Weeping_noises]

1293
00:44:00,038 --> 00:44:01,507
-I lost my retainer.

1294
00:44:05,543 --> 00:44:07,680
-Oh, they were-- they were
thinking Julie, go away.

1295
00:44:10,382 --> 00:44:12,017
-(SINGING) Because
I'm the wanderer,

1296
00:44:12,050 --> 00:44:14,018
they call me the wanderer.

1297
00:44:14,052 --> 00:44:16,855
-The Butcher was walking
slowly toward Los Angeles.

1298
00:44:16,888 --> 00:44:17,990
-The killer awoke before dawn.

1299
00:44:18,023 --> 00:44:19,525
-He traveled
through wooded hills

1300
00:44:19,558 --> 00:44:22,460
to prevent being seen by anyone.

1301
00:44:22,493 --> 00:44:24,597
Finally, he came
to a quiet road.

1302
00:44:24,630 --> 00:44:26,798
He stopped to get his bearings.

1303
00:44:26,832 --> 00:44:27,833
-Uh, whose woods these?

1304
00:44:27,867 --> 00:44:29,535
I think no.

1305
00:44:29,568 --> 00:44:31,337
-He saw something else.

1306
00:44:31,370 --> 00:44:33,005
-Jessica Rabbit!

1307
00:44:33,038 --> 00:44:34,907
-For the Butcher,
that rang a bell.

1308
00:44:34,940 --> 00:44:36,442
-I get it, I get it,
I get it, I get it.

1309
00:44:36,475 --> 00:44:37,408
I do.

1310
00:44:37,442 --> 00:44:38,343
-With a car, he knew
by the next afternoon

1311
00:44:38,377 --> 00:44:40,445
he'd be in Los Angeles
in plenty of time

1312
00:44:40,478 --> 00:44:43,882
to catch up with the three men
who had double-crossed him.

1313
00:44:43,915 --> 00:44:46,685
-Say, if I had a car, I could
be in Los Angeles in time

1314
00:44:46,718 --> 00:44:48,753
to catch up to the three
men who double-crossed me!

1315
00:44:52,457 --> 00:44:57,362
-Sooo, uhh, live
around here much?

1316
00:44:57,395 --> 00:44:58,763
-Well, see, you're
a real gentleman

1317
00:44:58,797 --> 00:45:00,398
to be helping
strangers in distress.

1318
00:45:00,432 --> 00:45:02,400
I hope you don't mind me
using the babe here as bait.

1319
00:45:02,433 --> 00:45:03,468
Quite a dish, isn't she?

1320
00:45:03,502 --> 00:45:04,302
-Hey!

1321
00:45:04,335 --> 00:45:04,870
-But that's what
she gets paid for--

1322
00:45:04,903 --> 00:45:05,303
-I can't believe this.

1323
00:45:05,337 --> 00:45:06,272
-Babe, that is.

1324
00:45:06,305 --> 00:45:08,740
Yes sir, she's a real
shaker of my outfit.

1325
00:45:08,773 --> 00:45:09,775
Carnival man, myself.

1326
00:45:09,808 --> 00:45:10,775
Just call me Carny.

1327
00:45:10,808 --> 00:45:11,477
-Makes me sick.

1328
00:45:11,510 --> 00:45:11,977
-Art Carney.

1329
00:45:11,977 --> 00:45:13,078
-Yeah.

1330
00:45:13,078 --> 00:45:15,480
Well, friend, we're in a
little bit difficulty, here.

1331
00:45:15,513 --> 00:45:18,083
Got a flat tire, brand new
car, no tools in the back.

1332
00:45:18,117 --> 00:45:19,050
Why, some of these dealers--

1333
00:45:19,083 --> 00:45:20,318
-He does go on.

1334
00:45:20,352 --> 00:45:21,887
--make my my carnival business
look like babes in the woods.

1335
00:45:24,457 --> 00:45:25,023
-Indestructible jerk.

1336
00:45:29,595 --> 00:45:31,597
-I lo-- love this car.

1337
00:45:36,502 --> 00:45:37,468
-Huh.

1338
00:45:37,502 --> 00:45:40,038
-The time pedal, in color.

1339
00:45:40,072 --> 00:45:44,810
Duh-- Tony, where are you?

1340
00:45:44,843 --> 00:45:46,778
-My back!

1341
00:45:46,845 --> 00:45:48,080
-Say, who you with, friend?

1342
00:45:48,113 --> 00:45:48,480
And let it down!

1343
00:45:48,513 --> 00:45:49,582
Let it down.

1344
00:45:49,615 --> 00:45:50,015
-I'm with AAA.

1345
00:45:53,018 --> 00:45:55,053
-No matter who you're
with, friend-- believe me,

1346
00:45:55,087 --> 00:45:55,987
I can do better for both of us.

1347
00:45:56,020 --> 00:45:58,857
I'll make a fortune
for the both of us.

1348
00:45:58,890 --> 00:46:00,125
-Oops.

1349
00:46:00,158 --> 00:46:02,962
-The silent scream.

1350
00:46:02,995 --> 00:46:05,963
-Uh, I'll write up
the contract, then.

1351
00:46:05,997 --> 00:46:07,498
-Be careful, there's
a three on the tree,

1352
00:46:07,532 --> 00:46:08,633
and don't ride the clutch!

1353
00:46:13,272 --> 00:46:14,338
-Lie detector by Mattel.

1354
00:46:14,372 --> 00:46:16,842
-An hour later, the San
Francisco police department

1355
00:46:16,875 --> 00:46:19,878
sent out an APB.

1356
00:46:19,912 --> 00:46:20,612
-Let me see.

1357
00:46:20,678 --> 00:46:22,280
-Big typewriters
were brought in,

1358
00:46:22,313 --> 00:46:23,448
and people were fed into them.

1359
00:46:23,482 --> 00:46:25,617
-Squads were sent to various
parts of the countryside.

1360
00:46:25,650 --> 00:46:27,920
-Scrods?

1361
00:46:27,953 --> 00:46:31,823
-(SINGING IN HARMONY)
Car 54, where are you?

1362
00:46:31,857 --> 00:46:33,192
-Oh, there it is.

1363
00:46:33,225 --> 00:46:36,295
-Higway patrol, starring
Broderick Crawford.

1364
00:46:36,328 --> 00:46:38,563
-Be on the lookout
for doughnuts.

1365
00:46:38,597 --> 00:46:39,330
-Oh, come on, Joel.

1366
00:46:39,363 --> 00:46:40,198
-APB for doughnuts.

1367
00:46:40,232 --> 00:46:41,200
-That's really lame.

1368
00:46:41,233 --> 00:46:42,700
No more of these doughnut jokes.

1369
00:46:42,733 --> 00:46:44,670
-(Giggles)

1370
00:46:44,703 --> 00:46:48,273
-Oh, pardon me, Tom "I've Got
To Take An Indestructible Wiz"

1371
00:46:48,307 --> 00:46:48,973
Servo.

1372
00:46:49,007 --> 00:46:50,008
-(THREATENTING) Why, I oughta--

1373
00:46:58,083 --> 00:46:59,383
-So, the sirens of Titan, huh?

1374
00:46:59,417 --> 00:47:00,585
-Kurt Vonnegut.

1375
00:47:03,255 --> 00:47:05,123
-Oh, it's The Cannonball Run!

1376
00:47:05,157 --> 00:47:06,392
-[Cackles]

1377
00:47:06,425 --> 00:47:08,760
-Roadblocks were thrown up to
catch the cold-blooded killer

1378
00:47:08,793 --> 00:47:11,028
the hysterical
girl had described.

1379
00:47:11,062 --> 00:47:12,663
-Excuse me, sir.

1380
00:47:12,697 --> 00:47:13,532
You a killer?

1381
00:47:13,565 --> 00:47:15,133
-The one the hysterical
girl described?

1382
00:47:15,167 --> 00:47:17,202
-And each of their
stories was the same.

1383
00:47:17,235 --> 00:47:20,038
So far, none of them had spotted
the car the killer was driving.

1384
00:47:20,072 --> 00:47:20,905
-Sam Snead!

1385
00:47:20,938 --> 00:47:22,975
-Orders were given
to shoot to kill.

1386
00:47:23,008 --> 00:47:25,010
-Shoot it down, shoot to kill.

1387
00:47:25,043 --> 00:47:27,078
-They realized they were
dealing with a maniac.

1388
00:47:27,112 --> 00:47:29,782
-Who was dancing like
he's never danced before.

1389
00:47:34,018 --> 00:47:35,320
-Whoa.

1390
00:47:35,353 --> 00:47:39,925
-Yeah, I'd like to
see your license, sir.

1391
00:47:39,958 --> 00:47:40,758
-Oh.

1392
00:47:40,792 --> 00:47:42,928
-Take it out of
the wallet, please.

1393
00:47:42,962 --> 00:47:45,263
Sir?

1394
00:47:45,297 --> 00:47:45,897
-Huh.

1395
00:47:48,867 --> 00:47:49,768
-[Screams]

1396
00:47:49,802 --> 00:47:50,702
-Huh, mind if I play
through? [CHUCKLES]

1397
00:47:50,735 --> 00:47:51,270
-Sam Snead!

1398
00:47:55,740 --> 00:47:57,910
-Huh, well, I suppose
they do those things.

1399
00:48:02,982 --> 00:48:03,915
-Money.

1400
00:48:03,948 --> 00:48:05,017
Revenge.

1401
00:48:05,050 --> 00:48:05,883
Money.

1402
00:48:05,917 --> 00:48:06,752
Revenge.

1403
00:48:06,785 --> 00:48:07,920
[Honks]

1404
00:48:07,953 --> 00:48:09,888
-Honk if you're indestructible.

1405
00:48:09,922 --> 00:48:12,090
-If you were indestructible,
you'd be home by now.

1406
00:48:16,060 --> 00:48:22,100
-Oh, Wall Drug, 2,000 miles.

1407
00:48:22,133 --> 00:48:23,902
-Gee, if I could only read.

1408
00:48:23,935 --> 00:48:27,238
(Guffaws)

1409
00:48:27,272 --> 00:48:30,108
-Yeah, he's indestructible,
but he needs a light wrap,

1410
00:48:30,142 --> 00:48:30,808
you know?

1411
00:48:30,842 --> 00:48:31,943
-Right, well the boy gets cold.

1412
00:48:31,977 --> 00:48:35,047
-More for looks.

1413
00:48:35,080 --> 00:48:35,880
-Hall of Justice.

1414
00:48:35,913 --> 00:48:39,050
Over 1,000,000 served.

1415
00:48:39,083 --> 00:48:40,418
-[Imitates_background_music]

1416
00:48:40,452 --> 00:48:43,155
-Captain J. I. Estee Lauder.

1417
00:48:43,188 --> 00:48:46,958
-I trust this information will
answer all of your questions.

1418
00:48:46,992 --> 00:48:49,327
If I can be of any further
service, let me know.

1419
00:48:49,360 --> 00:48:50,928
-Your total is $4.35.

1420
00:48:50,962 --> 00:48:53,065
Please pull ahead.

1421
00:48:53,098 --> 00:48:55,833
-Another all-points
from CHB, Captain.

1422
00:48:55,867 --> 00:48:57,202
-Eh, we got this
bill from Winchell's.

1423
00:48:57,235 --> 00:48:59,972
Uh, it's in the six digits,
so you have to sign off.

1424
00:49:00,005 --> 00:49:03,208
-Wait, who's ordering
the specialty doughnuts?

1425
00:49:03,242 --> 00:49:04,242
Hold it a second.

1426
00:49:04,275 --> 00:49:05,010
I--

1427
00:49:05,043 --> 00:49:06,110
-Hit him.

1428
00:49:06,143 --> 00:49:07,512
-Words sound like they come
from a bunch of loonies.

1429
00:49:07,545 --> 00:49:09,213
-Well, that's that
Barney Miller.

1430
00:49:09,247 --> 00:49:10,115
-There it is.

1431
00:49:10,148 --> 00:49:10,615
-Sergeant.

1432
00:49:10,648 --> 00:49:11,617
-Yeah.

1433
00:49:11,650 --> 00:49:12,985
-I want Lieutenant
Chasen to look these over

1434
00:49:13,018 --> 00:49:13,952
as soon as he comes in.

1435
00:49:13,985 --> 00:49:14,887
-All right, Captain.

1436
00:49:14,920 --> 00:49:16,020
-It's some poetry
I've been working on.

1437
00:49:19,190 --> 00:49:20,458
-The next morning,
I read the stories

1438
00:49:20,492 --> 00:49:23,062
of the brutal murder
of two police officers.

1439
00:49:23,095 --> 00:49:23,895
-Really picked me up.

1440
00:49:23,928 --> 00:49:25,563
-I also read the
witness's statement

1441
00:49:25,597 --> 00:49:28,033
about the bullets having
no effect upon the killer.

1442
00:49:28,067 --> 00:49:30,002
-Sunny and 84 today.

1443
00:49:30,035 --> 00:49:32,037
New petitions against tax.

1444
00:49:32,070 --> 00:49:34,072
-I felt sorry for
the witness, and I

1445
00:49:34,105 --> 00:49:36,675
thought about how easily
a person's mind can become

1446
00:49:36,708 --> 00:49:40,445
confused when suddenly they
receive a brutal shock.

1447
00:49:40,478 --> 00:49:41,513
-Then I went to lunch.

1448
00:49:41,547 --> 00:49:43,082
-I couldn't believe a
man existed who could not

1449
00:49:43,115 --> 00:49:45,183
be stopped by a slug from a .45.

1450
00:49:45,217 --> 00:49:47,252
-(SINGING) Aruba, Jamaica--

1451
00:49:47,285 --> 00:49:48,053
-Good morning, Captain.

1452
00:49:48,087 --> 00:49:49,653
-Oh excuse me.

1453
00:49:49,688 --> 00:49:52,290
-You and the rest of the force
are going on 24 hour duty.

1454
00:49:52,323 --> 00:49:54,292
-All right, yeah-- oh.

1455
00:49:54,325 --> 00:49:55,460
-Headlines?

1456
00:49:55,493 --> 00:49:57,395
-This guy that's
running amok up north.

1457
00:49:57,428 --> 00:49:59,097
-Yeah, and he's
running amok, too.

1458
00:49:59,130 --> 00:50:01,032
-Last report, he's killed
two police officers.

1459
00:50:01,065 --> 00:50:03,302
Made the getaway in a
green Coop heading south.

1460
00:50:03,335 --> 00:50:05,503
-When he gets up to four police
officers, we'll do something.

1461
00:50:05,537 --> 00:50:07,238
-I've got a hunch this
killer's gonna be our baby.

1462
00:50:07,272 --> 00:50:09,673
-I noticed you knitted
a little blue holster.

1463
00:50:09,708 --> 00:50:11,075
-Yeah.

1464
00:50:11,108 --> 00:50:12,377
-Better get started, Lieutenant.

1465
00:50:12,410 --> 00:50:12,977
-Ok.

1466
00:50:13,010 --> 00:50:13,912
-Ok.

1467
00:50:13,945 --> 00:50:14,813
Now I gotta stop at
Bargain Clown first.

1468
00:50:14,847 --> 00:50:17,148
-Any new leads on
that hold-up money?

1469
00:50:17,182 --> 00:50:19,617
-I think the girl can
probably be counted out.

1470
00:50:19,650 --> 00:50:23,055
If any of the others are wise,
they're keep mighty quiet.

1471
00:50:23,088 --> 00:50:24,555
-Let it ride for now.

1472
00:50:24,588 --> 00:50:26,725
The whole force is
going to be on the spot

1473
00:50:26,758 --> 00:50:27,592
until we catch this killer.

1474
00:50:27,625 --> 00:50:29,527
-They're doing "Camelot"
across the lot.

1475
00:50:29,560 --> 00:50:30,228
-[Humming]

1476
00:50:30,262 --> 00:50:30,862
-Newspaper'll have a field day.

1477
00:50:30,895 --> 00:50:32,563
-Hey, shut up over there!

1478
00:50:32,597 --> 00:50:34,198
[Humming continues]

1479
00:50:37,168 --> 00:50:38,737
-Mm, Grandma.

1480
00:50:38,770 --> 00:50:41,105
-Hey, who says oldsters
can't stay active?

1481
00:50:41,138 --> 00:50:42,707
-Gray lady down.

1482
00:50:42,740 --> 00:50:43,442
-All hands on deck.

1483
00:50:43,475 --> 00:50:44,142
-[Wolf_whistles]

1484
00:50:44,175 --> 00:50:44,977
-We were wrong about two things.

1485
00:50:45,010 --> 00:50:47,645
The killer was
already in our city,

1486
00:50:47,678 --> 00:50:50,348
and we didn't know he
was Butcher Benton.

1487
00:50:50,382 --> 00:50:52,450
He headed straight
for Eva Martin.

1488
00:50:52,483 --> 00:50:54,218
He went to her dressing
room to get back

1489
00:50:54,285 --> 00:50:56,822
the map he had left in
the envelope with her.

1490
00:50:56,855 --> 00:50:58,657
He wanted first to
check his money,

1491
00:50:58,690 --> 00:51:00,092
then catch up with
Lowe and the others.

1492
00:51:00,125 --> 00:51:01,192
-And if anybody's
interested, he's

1493
00:51:01,225 --> 00:51:04,095
gotta pick up his shirts
at the cleaners later, too.

1494
00:51:04,128 --> 00:51:05,530
-Hey, I'm voting in here.

1495
00:51:05,563 --> 00:51:06,632
-Oh.

1496
00:51:06,665 --> 00:51:08,433
-(SPUTTERING) But
I was, and then,

1497
00:51:08,467 --> 00:51:10,602
you were dead with the-- ohh.

1498
00:51:10,635 --> 00:51:13,672
-Honey, I'm home.

1499
00:51:13,705 --> 00:51:14,840
-(IMITATING FEMALE
VOICE) Oh, well.

1500
00:51:14,873 --> 00:51:16,808
-It can't be.

1501
00:51:16,842 --> 00:51:18,777
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE):
I know I'm a little under--

1502
00:51:18,810 --> 00:51:22,547
over-- uh, I don't know
how I'm dressed, but--

1503
00:51:22,580 --> 00:51:23,782
-You do look like him.

1504
00:51:23,815 --> 00:51:25,683
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) And
you do smell like him, too.

1505
00:51:31,422 --> 00:51:32,757
-Carl Balentine?

1506
00:51:32,790 --> 00:51:34,292
-McHale.

1507
00:51:34,325 --> 00:51:37,562
-It is you.

1508
00:51:37,595 --> 00:51:38,830
-Why didn't somebody tell me?

1509
00:51:41,867 --> 00:51:43,535
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
No, but you're not now.

1510
00:51:43,568 --> 00:51:46,472
I need to be up right now.

1511
00:51:46,505 --> 00:51:47,705
-Those look like bullet holes.

1512
00:51:47,738 --> 00:51:49,540
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) Well,
I can patch that up easily.

1513
00:51:49,573 --> 00:51:50,875
I have a needle and thread.

1514
00:51:54,578 --> 00:51:56,347
-There's not even a mark.

1515
00:51:56,380 --> 00:51:58,283
-Hm.

1516
00:51:58,317 --> 00:52:00,285
-Darling, out of the way.

1517
00:52:05,923 --> 00:52:08,693
-Uh, I think
scissors beats hand.

1518
00:52:08,727 --> 00:52:10,928
-Yeah.

1519
00:52:10,962 --> 00:52:11,863
-See there?

1520
00:52:11,897 --> 00:52:12,463
Look, get it?

1521
00:52:12,497 --> 00:52:13,698
No ow.

1522
00:52:13,732 --> 00:52:15,767
Ok?

1523
00:52:15,800 --> 00:52:16,400
-Hmm.

1524
00:52:20,605 --> 00:52:21,372
Now watch this.

1525
00:52:21,405 --> 00:52:22,873
No paper cuts!

1526
00:52:22,907 --> 00:52:25,843
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) But
you speak in riddles, Sahib.

1527
00:52:25,877 --> 00:52:30,248
-I opened it like you told
me, and found the $50.

1528
00:52:30,282 --> 00:52:30,915
-I had $51!

1529
00:52:30,948 --> 00:52:33,318
What happened to my dollar?

1530
00:52:33,352 --> 00:52:35,720
-I didn't know why he left it.

1531
00:52:35,753 --> 00:52:37,788
Paul didn't know
why, and he was here!

1532
00:52:37,822 --> 00:52:39,357
-Oh, focus!

1533
00:52:39,390 --> 00:52:39,757
-Whoa.

1534
00:52:39,790 --> 00:52:40,292
-Hey!

1535
00:52:40,325 --> 00:52:41,593
-Lay's potato chips.

1536
00:52:41,627 --> 00:52:43,328
No one could eat just one.

1537
00:52:47,432 --> 00:52:50,802
-What am I doing?

1538
00:52:50,835 --> 00:52:52,803
-Don't-- don't get crazy.

1539
00:52:52,837 --> 00:52:56,507
-[Growls]

1540
00:52:56,542 --> 00:52:58,510
-The name of Paul
triggered his hate.

1541
00:52:58,543 --> 00:52:59,810
-The name Gary made him happy!

1542
00:52:59,843 --> 00:53:00,945
